he exchange rate start to reflect market forces. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.0% at 9331.22 midday, as big banks lead gainers after Basel Committee's new capital adequacy ratio rules were less stringent than expected, traders say; 9400 index resistance level may remain for rest of day. "Some investors are staying on the sidelines before the Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election tomorrow (TSE 1st section volume just 700 million shares), but overall sentiment is good today," says Hikaru Sato, senior technical analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. MUFG (8306.TO) up 2.2% at Y412, \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 +1.5% at Y132. Shippers also higher, with \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) up 2.7% to Y346 on Nikkei report that maritime shipping rates for iron ore hitting 3-month high. Slightly weaker yen benefiting exporters generally; \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 2.9% at Y4,270, Honda (7267.TO) up 1.9% at Y2,838. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +1.2% at 21,509.02 tracking Wall Street cues, but further upside eyed capped at 22,000 near term. Taifook notes strong China August CPI data released at weekend well-anticipated, not having impact on local stocks; says "the broader market's key features would continue to be rotational buying and range trading in the near term." Among leading gainers, HKEx (0388.HK) +1.6% at HK$136.70 as local bourse extends recent strength, while Cnooc (0883.HK) +2.0% at HK$14.42 on higher oil prices. Volume brisk at HK$9.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower after PBOC adviser Li Daokui says on Sunday that China ought to raise deposit interest rates slightly, following strong August CPI data. "Since last week, there has been speculation among investors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might hike interest rates imminently to curb inflation. Such concerns have hurt the overall sentiment in the local bond market," says Shanghai-based trader with local brokerage. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.64 vs 126.67 last close, tipped to test short-term support of 126.5 in coming sessions. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds lower, weighed by weaker U.S. Treasurys Friday, Taiex's 1.2% gain, says local brokerage trader. Still, notes volume thin as traders stay on sidelines ahead of new 10-year bond auction Wednesday. Expects weak demand at 10-year bond auction as "Taiwan is likely to hike key policy rates again this month, and also on recent strong stock market." Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet September 30. 10-year yield last at 1.2415% in when-issued trade vs 1.2253% yesterday, eyed in 1.2350%-1.2550% band for day. 5-year yield last at 0.8950% vs 0.8850% yesterday, eyed in 0.8850%-0.9050% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian banks should be able to comfortably meet common equity tier 1 and total tier 1 requirements coming from Basel committee, though questions remain, says Macquarie research. Adds Australian banks tier 1 ratios will likely be at least 10% under new guidelines, with banks able to support lower levels of credit growth as a result. But notes "uncertainty remains as to whether or not banks will modify payouts to avoid a situation where they could eat into the capital conservation buffer." Next up locally, APRA will take next year to work through proposals and adapt for Australia. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares set new record high in early trade, playing catch up from Friday's gains on Wall Street (local markets closed Friday for holiday); benchmark PSEi +1.1% at 3942.07 in moderate volume; immediate resistance 3950, then 4000, support at 3900. Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities, says foreign fund inflows due to strong corporate, economic outlook continue to drive market to uncharted territory. "The peso's appreciation is also helping sentiment," he adds. Advancers led by property, utility stocks such as PLDT (TEL.PH) +0.6% at PHP2,420, Megaworld (MEG.PH) +4.7% at PHP2.23, SM Investments (SM.PH) +1.2% at PHP516, Filinvest Land (FLI.PH) +2.9% at PHP1.40 and \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH) +2.7% at PHP26.30. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 09-12-10 2306ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100913e69d0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
