\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Up 1.2%; Extends Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 1,675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 04:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0235 GMT misstated the extent of the market high. The correct version is as follows:) S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 3-month high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan stocks up sharply across board; Taiex last +1.2% at 7986.51, but meets strong resistance at 8000 after trading in 7946.98-7995.35 range in morning. Capital Securities' analyst Diana Wu says Taiex already broke immediate resistance at 7950, as investor sentiment buoyed by ECFA (trade pact with China) which took effect Sunday; tips China plays, textile, financial stocks to outperform broader market. China Airlines (2610.TW) +4.4% at NT$20.10 ahead of officially joining \uc2 SkyTeam\uc2  Alliance Tuesday, smaller rival \uc2 EVA Airways\uc2  (2618.TW) also +5.2% at NT$24.10. Hon Hai (2317.TW) +3.24% after posting August revenue doubled from year earlier, TSMC (2330.TW) also +1.4% at NT$60.10. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 4-week high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Geithner's remark made in WSJ interview Friday that China has not done enough in terms of CNY appreciation presents another hurdle to Japan stepping in to slow rising JPY says Hideki Amikura, senior dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking. "Even for a solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you need at least an O.K. sign from the country of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  you plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in, in this case the U.S." But getting such go-ahead from U