\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares End Mostly Higher; China Data Boosts Sentiment\b0\par\par\uc2 982 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2010\par 12:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were mostly upbeat on Wednesday as solid economic data from Australia and China helped investors overcome worries over the health of the global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Investors were heartened by China manufacturing data showing growth in the world's second largest economy is stabilizing at a moderate but healthy pace, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "This reconfirmed our long-held view that China is moderating rather than melting down," wrote \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's co-head of Asian economics research Qu Hongbin.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average ended 1.2% higher, rebounding after touching a new 16-month low earlier, while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 rose 2.1%. The Shanghai Composite ended 0.6% lower, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index added 0.4%. Thailand's SET index tacked on 0.7% to end at 919.34, its highest level since December 1996.\par \par \uc2 Australian shares surged after data from the Australian Bureau of Statistics showed the country's average measure of GDP rose a better-than-expected 1.2% in the second quarter of 2010 from the first quarter, and grew 3.3% from the year-earlier period.\par \par \uc2 Heavyweight financials and materials sectors extended gains after the data, with BHP Billiton was 2.3% higher and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  added 3.1%. Banking shares also recorded sizeable gains.\par \par \uc2 In China, figures released Wednesday showed the country's official Purchasing Managers Index rose to 51.7 in August from 51.2 in July. A separate PMI put out by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  and compiled by Markit, rose to 51.9 from 49.4, rebounding above the 50 threshold level and into growth after readings in July indicated the first contraction in manufacturing activity in 16 months.\par \par \uc2 Still, the news failed to help support China's Shanghai Composite, as declines in small and medium-sized companies, which had seen sizeable gains throughout the summer, dragged the index into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 Real-estate developers also fell after China's banking regulator reiterated Tuesday it will firmly curb speculative investment in the sector. Ye Yanfei, a deputy director at the China Banking Regulatory Commission's statistics department, said the property market and property loans are crucial to the national economic development and important to the stability of the banking industry.\par \par \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  slid 1.3% and Gemdale was down 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 The encouraging data from China helped to bolster Japanese stocks, with exporters' shares getting an added fillip from a pledge by Democratic Party of Japan presidential candidate Ichiro Ozawa to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the yen's steep rise if elected party leader. Ozawa has formally filed his candidacy for the DPJ presidential election on Sept. 14, when he will vie with incumbent leader and Prime Minister Naoto Kan.\par \par \uc2 "There have been widespread doubts over whether the current government can really carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities. "The specific mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (by Ozawa) had an impact."\par \par \uc2 Among exporters, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  added 1.0%, Canon tacked on 1.2% and Olympus rose 3.0%.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Gas\uc2  was up 1.0% after it said Tuesday that it will buy a 1.5% stake in \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K\uc2 . Lines' liquefied natural gas vessel \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K\uc2 . added 1.0%.\par \par \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  was up 2.5% and Mitsubishi Estate rose 3.3% after data Tuesday showed Japan's housing starts in July rose 4.3% from the year before, up for the second-consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's shares climbed to a 14-year high after data showed inflation slowed in August, with the consumer price index rising 3.3% from a year earlier, compared with a 3.4% rise in July. The data supported analysts' expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may only increase interest rates once more this year. Banks advanced, with \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  rising 1.4% and \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  tacking on 4.6%.\par \par \uc2 Among other regional markets, Taiwan's Taiex added 0.7%. South Korea's Kospi Composite added 1.3%, New Zealand's NZX-50 was up 1.4%, India's Sensex gained 1.3%, Singapore's Straits Times Index gained 1.1%, Philippine shares were up 0.8%, Indonesia shares rose 1.7% and Malaysia's KLCI added 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Japanese yen fell back slightly against the U.S. dollar and the euro, as demand subsided after the upbeat data from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Australia and after Ozawa's remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was at Y83.99, from Y83.83 in late New York trade Tuesday, while the euro was at Y107.47, from Y106.25.\par \par \uc2 The euro was trading at $1.2794 against the dollar, from $1.2673, boosted by increased risk appetite after the better economic data.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rose after the strong GDP data, trading at US$0.9046, up from US$0.8898 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  economist Dominic Bryant said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  will likely sound more upbeat at its policy meeting next week, with the strong GDP numbers keeping them on track for a possible interest rate hike in November.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,249.60 per troy ounce, up $1.90 from late New York trade on Tuesday. October Nymex crude oil futures were up 62 cents at $72.54 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 -Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; markettalk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 01-09-10 1038GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100901e691000sy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
