\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Australia Opposition To Repair China Ties -FOCUS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 08:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's main opposition bloc is vowing to re-engage key Asian trade partners, particularly China--upset by diplomatic dust-ups in the past year--if it wins the Aug. 21 federal election. But in office they may encounter same difficulties faced by center-left Labor: especially as conservative voices within coalition favor banning investment in Australian resources by state-owned firms. Julie Bishop, the coalition's foreign affairs spokeswoman, promises a "reinvigorated diplomatic effort" after 3 years of "untidy and sometimes chaotic behavior on the part of the government". In its defense, Labor doesn't believe it damaged ties with Asia in its first term. Still, China experts say Australia is viewed by China as a "difficult jurisdiction" for deals since Labor announced plans for a profits-based tax on mining companies in May. Latest polls show election will likely be a photo finish. See FOCUS: Australia Opposition Bloc Vows To Repair China Ties(rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0617 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bond auction cutoffs likely in-line with secondary market levels, but some signs demand may be dampened by recent RBI rule asking banks to declare sale or transfer of bonds from Held To Maturity (HTM)category over 5%, 4 traders say. Cut-off yield for benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond estimated at 7.90% according to median forecast of 5 dealers in Dow Jones poll, while cut-off for 7.17% 2015 bond seen at 7.70%. Tip yield cut-off for longer tenor 8.26% 2027 bond at 8.40%; however, trader with foreign bank remains optimistic on demand at auction. "If the HTM rule issue was a deterrent, bonds would probably have sold off more earlier this week," trader says. RBI to sell INR120 billion of government bonds later in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD extends gains as Chinese banks buy on corporate demand, says trader at U.K. bank. Tips pair at 7.7650 -7.7700 band near term. "Still, I've spotted some market players starting to sell the U.S. dollar near 7.7700 as the firmer local stocks pull investors from selling the Hong Kong dollar," trader says. HSI +0.1% midday. Pair last at 7.7695 vs 7.7683 this morning, compared with 7.7671 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] A play in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to be long both commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as AUD, NZD, CAD, and long safe-havens, such as JPY and CHF, has to break soon, with commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to break lower, says Joseph Capurso, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 . "Equity markets will soften and risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fall away, leaving the Aussie, Kiwi and CAD falling away for the next little while. But that doesn't mean I think the Aussie is doomed," says Capurso. AUD/USD recently at 0.9023, up from 0.8974 late yesterday, with Capurso tipping support at 0.8800 should decline begin. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says the timing of Italy's tap auction of the BTP 3% June 2015 and the BTP 5% March 2025 Friday is "tricky," given renewed tension in the periphery. The timing of Ireland's auction of 4% January 2014 and 5% October 2020 bonds Aug. 17, meanwhile, "could be even more challenging," says strategist David Schnautz. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely up in near term as rising current account deficit, sluggish capital flows to weigh on INR, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in report. "Indeed, the RBI also noted that risk from a potential slowdown in capital inflows is significant," house says. Tips 3-month projection of pair at 47.50 vs last at 46.64. 6- and 12-month projections for pair at 44.00 and 43.00 driven by significant USD weakness. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] For Aussie bond traders, concern about U.S. trumps all over global factors right now, even moving past anything in Europe, says senior Sydney-based bond trader. "It's all about what Treasurys will do and we'll just follow along after that," says trader. Adds, outperformance of Aussie bonds vs U.S. Treasurys -- which happened overnight -- likely to continue in short term, with RBA holding pattern keeping bond action more muted domestically. For Friday, action mixed, with 3-year down 1 tick at 95.42, 10-year up 1 tick at 94.985. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi extends gains, last +1.2% at 1742.70 as investors buy tech, shipping, commodity plays after recent falls, says Park Jung-je at Taurus Investment & Securities. Thinks benchmark index will close near 1740 today, tips 1730-1760 band for next week as 2Q GDP numbers in European countries including Germany, France due tonight "likely to be strong despite uncertainties over European economic growth. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +1.4% at KRW777,000, STX Pan Ocean (028670.SE) +3.4% at KRW12,150, \uc2 SK Energy Co\uc2 . (096770.SE) +2.4% at KRW127,000. But bank stocks weak; \uc2 KB Financial Group Inc\uc2 . (105560.SE) off 1.1% at KRW48,200, \uc2 Hana Financial Group\uc2  (086790.SE) down 2.0% at KRW32,600. Foreigners net sellers in local bourse today, selling KRW123.96 billion worth of stocks; but institutions, retail investors buying KRW28.86 billion, KRW132.97 billion worth of stocks, respectively. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 GMT [Dow Jones] India's June industry output growth in single digits may not alter \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy stance (of gradual tightening) as inflation still at elevated levels, says Standard Chartered Bank. Industrial production +7.1% on-year in June, sharply lower than May's +11.3%. "We expect a calibrated increase of 75 basis points in the repo rate by Q1-2011 from the current 5.75%," it says. Adds industry output to settle in single digits going forward as lower base vanishes. Industrial activity to moderate from feverish pace but no indication of collapse in activity. "However, month-on-month gains in industrial activity should be smaller going forward, as business optimism has plateaued," it says. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ may increase size of liquidity provisions and/or extend funding terms from current 3 months if JPY strengthens further, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 ; but move may not be right prescription as JPY Libor already meaningfully lower than USD Libor for 3-, 6-months and U.S. developments, in particular significantly lower Treasury yields, are main driver for USD/JPY moves. Alternatively, BOJ could expand the newly-introduced "fund-provisioning measure to support strengthening the foundation for economic growth," under which BOJ provides 1-year loans to private financial institutions with policy rates, when they make loans and investment to "growth areas," though Citi says impact of this also probably very modest for the same reasons. Says increase in outright purchases of long-term JGBs likely to have much stronger impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "However, the BOJ has been reluctant to take this action in the past until there emerge compelling signs of a relapse of economic activity or until there (is) significant turmoil in financial markets that worsens overall financial conditions." USD/JPY last at 86.07. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , even though government appears to have heightened vigilance on JPY strength. Notes 2 psychological hurdles - "First, they probably think that other industrialized counties, especially the U.S., are seeking weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to support their economies and prevent deflation and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be problematic in this context." Also, MoF likely believes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is difficult when international community is pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase CNY flexibility. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects benchmark 3-month Hibor to stay low near 0.30% from now until 2012 as it expects first U.S. Fed hike to come only in 2Q 2012 amid uncertain U.S. economic recovery. "We believe that the Hong Kong (and China) recovery story will remain fundamentally compelling to global investors," which will help to lure inflows, keeping interbank liquidity ample, report says. Expects Hibors to start climbing only in early 2012. 3-month Hibor steady at 0.28%.(chester.yung@dowjones.com) [ 08-13-10 0218ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100813e68d0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
