gher as banks cover cash requirements for weekend, but rate may turn lower as most lenders have already covered positions on account of reporting Friday, says trader with state-run bank. Banks have to report cash reserve levels to RBI at end of every fortnight. Overnight call rate at 5.76% vs 5.25% last close. Collateralized lending and borrowing obligation, or CBLO, rate slips to 4.61% vs 5.00% at open, banks avoid borrowing from CBLO window as they have to pledge bonds as collateral. "The RBI has made available two liquidity adjustment facility windows on reporting Fridays, when cash positions of most banks are covered well in advance, so borrowing rates may be subdued," trader says. Tips call rate may slip to 5.00% in session while CBLO support tipped at 3.75%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] China's banking regulator may be losing faith in the country's trust companies, says Heard on the Street, another amber light for foreign institutions considering investing in this sector; increased scrutiny by the CBRC comes amid worries over practice of banks selling loans to trust companies so that they can repackage them and sell them on to investors. That threatens steady source of income for trust companies, which at forefront of financial services innovation in China; part of trusts' appeal till now has been their relative freedom from regulatory scrutiny, but that seems to be changing. See "HEARD ON THE STREET: Losing Faith In China's Trust Companies" (andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0458 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW turns lower in afternoon trade on back of improved sentiment in regional stock markets, pushing Kospi higher (last up 1.1%), traders say. Local bank trader tips pair to target 1,182; "if Chinese shares extend gains later, it will be enough to push the pair down to that low level." But another local bank trader expects downside to be cushioned by foreign selling of local shares, while "exporter selling of dollars isn't as strong as it was yesterday." Pair last tad down at 1,185.2 vs 1,186.6 midday, 1,186.2 late yesterday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.2% at 9230.12 vs 9186.32 midday close (down 0.3%); volume low with Japanese "Obon" holiday season in full swing and as investors await string of U.S. economic data and action from Japanese government to stem yen's rise. "If it turns out that the government is just giving lip service (regarding strong yen), markets will test critical 9000 support," says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. Puts next week's Nikkei range at 9000-9300, noting index may rise to 9500 provided BOJ takes additional easing step(s). 18/33 Topix subindexes higher; some flight to domestic demand/defensive issues seen: \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) up 0.6% at Y12,890, Eisai (4523.TO) up 1.7% at Y2,994. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 1.6% at Y1,082 after GS ups to Buy. Many exporter shares continued to underperform; \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) down 1.2% at Y2,765, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 0.9% at Y9,600, \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) flat at Y1,495. CSK (9737.TO) down 8.8% at Y299 after brokerage target cut. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.1% at 21,120.46 midday, struggles to recover after index's 3-session 3.2% fall, amid uncertainty over global economic recovery after U.S. disappointing jobs data, says Daniel So at Sun Hung Kai Financial. "The index is now struggling at around its 250-day moving average (21,020) and it's unlikely to move much further up or down from that level, as there's still caution over the global economic outlook." Tips near-term support at 20,600. Still, expects upcoming corporate results of China banks, oil plays could help support trading sentiment in local market. Among these, ICBC (1398.HK) +0.2% at HK$5.70, Bank of China (3988.HK) +0.5% at HK$4.04, Cnooc (0883.HK) down 0.2% at HK$12.74, Sinopec (0386.HK) +0.7% at HK$6.15. Volume modest at HK$30.84 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 08-13-10 0147ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100813e68d0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
