\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kospi Extends Gains, +1.2%; Techs Lead Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 1,673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 07:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi extends gains, last +1.2% at 1742.70 as investors buy tech, shipping, commodity plays after recent falls, says Park Jung-je at Taurus Investment & Securities. Thinks benchmark index will close near 1740 today, tips 1730-1760 band for next week as 2Q GDP numbers in European countries including Germany, France due tonight "likely to be strong despite uncertainties over European economic growth. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +1.4% at KRW777,000, STX Pan Ocean (028670.SE) +3.4% at KRW12,150, \uc2 SK Energy Co\uc2 . (096770.SE) +2.4% at KRW127,000. But bank stocks weak; \uc2 KB Financial Group Inc\uc2 . (105560.SE) off 1.1% at KRW48,200, \uc2 Hana Financial Group\uc2  (086790.SE) down 2.0% at KRW32,600. Foreigners net sellers in local bourse today, selling KRW123.96 billion worth of stocks; but institutions, retail investors buying KRW28.86 billion, KRW132.97 billion worth of stocks, respectively. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 GMT [Dow Jones] India's June industry output growth in single digits may not alter \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy stance (of gradual tightening) as inflation still at elevated levels, says Standard Chartered Bank. Industrial production +7.1% on-year in June, sharply lower than May's +11.3%. "We expect a calibrated increase of 75 basis points in the repo rate by Q1-2011 from the current 5.75%," it says. Adds industry output to settle in single digits going forward as lower base vanishes. Industrial activity to moderate from feverish pace but no indication of collapse in activity. "However, month-on-month gains in industrial activity should be smaller going forward, as business optimism has plateaued," it says. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ may increase size of liquidity provisions and/or extend funding terms from current 3 months if JPY strengthens further, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 ; but move may not be right prescription as JPY Libor already meaningfully lower than USD Libor for 3-, 6-months and U.S. developments, in particular significantly lower Treasury yields, are main driver for USD/JPY moves. Alternatively, BOJ could expand the newly-introduced "fund-provisioning measure to support strengthening the foundation for economic growth," under which BOJ provides 1-year loans to private financial institutions with policy rates, when they make loans and investment to "growth areas," though Citi says impact of this also probably very modest for the same reasons. Says increase in outright purchases of long-term JGBs likely to have much stronger impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "However, the BOJ has been reluctant to take this action in the past until there emerge compelling signs of a relapse of economic activity or until there (is) significant turmoil in financial markets that worsens overall financial conditions." USD/JPY last at 86.07. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , even though government appears to have heightened vigilance on JPY strength. Notes 2 psychological hurdles - "First, they probably think that other industrialized counties, especially the U.S., are seeking weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to support their economies and prevent deflation and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be problematic in this context." Also, MoF likely believes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is difficult when international community is pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase CNY flexibility. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects benchmark 3-month Hibor to stay low near 0.30% from now until 2012 as it expects first U.S. Fed hike to come only in 2Q 2012 amid uncertain U.S. economic recovery. "We believe that the Hong Kong (and China) recovery story will remain fundamentally compelling to global investors," which will help to lure inflows, keeping interbank liquidity ample, report says. Expects Hibors to start climbing only in early 2012. 3-month Hibor steady at 0.28%.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate higher as banks cover cash requirements for weekend, but rate may turn lower as most lenders have already covered positions on account of reporting Friday, says trader with state-run bank. Banks have to report cash reserve levels to RBI at end of every fortnight. Overnight call rate at 5.76% vs 5.25% last close. Collateralized lending and borrowing obligation, or CBLO, rate slips to 4.61% vs 5.00% at open, banks avoid borrowing from CBLO window as they have to pledge bonds as collateral. "The RBI has made available two liquidity adjustment facility windows on reporting Fridays, when cash positions of most banks are covered well in advance, so borrowing rates may be subdued," trader says. Tips call rate may slip to 5.00% in session while CBLO support tipped at 3.75%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] China's banking regulator may be losing faith in the country's trust companies, says Heard on the Street, another amber light for foreign institutions considering investing in this sector; increased scrutiny by the CBRC comes amid worries over practice of banks se