\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China should widen yuan's float -c.bank researcher\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2010\par 03:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2  * Daily band should be widened to 1 pct -c.bank official\par \par \uc2  * C.bank adviser says yuan could fall if exports suffer  (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser, Xinhua)\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, July 22 (Reuters) - China should let the yuan float within a wider range in a gradual process over the next five years, an official at a local branch of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in comments published on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  Wang Yong, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's training school in Zhengzhou, Henan province, also said the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should scale back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so long as the yuan does not bump up against the limits imposed on its daily fluctuations.\par \par \uc2  The Chinese yuan can now move within a daily range of 0.5 percent up or down from a reference rate set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but has rarely approached those limits. It has gained only about 0.8 percent since Beijing removed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a de facto peg to the U.S. dollar on June 19.\par \par \uc2  The daily range should be expanded to 1 percent next year and 2 percent in 2015, Wang said in an article published in the official Shanghai Securities News.\par \par \uc2  While his comments do not necessarily reflect official thinking, they do highlight a range of differing views in China about how to reform the yuan's exchange rate regime.\par \par \uc2  Wang echoed the country's top leadership in saying that Beijing should resist any calls for rapid appreciation.\par \par \uc2  "China should not and cannot pay for the global financial crisis, and it has no reason to act according to other people's moods," he wrote.\par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke on Wednesday said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy effectively subsidizes its exports, giving fresh ammunition to China's critics in Congress who are pushing for legislation aimed at pressuring China to act more quickly. [ID:nN21183994]\par \par \uc2  Separately, Zhou Qiren, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser, said China could allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate against the U.S. dollar to support its exports if they are battered by the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2  Although last month's resumption of yuan appreciation has dominated recent headlines, China's more seminal reform came five years ago on July 21, 2005, when Beijing lifted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a formal peg to the dollar and set it on path of gradual appreciation.\par \par \uc2  In an editorial to mark the anniversary, the official Xinhua news agency said that Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform was making progress, with the exchange rate now clearly fluctuating in two directions.\par \par \uc2  (Reporting by Langi Chiang and Simon Rabinovitch; Editing by Ken Wills)     \par \par \uc2 CHINA-YUAN/BAND (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Z|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|ABX|RNP|DNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100722e67m00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
