\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK:Kospi +0.2% Amid Profit-Taking; Intel Results Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2010\par 07:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0508 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi maintaining slim gains, last up 0.2% at 1737.28 as market awaits Intel 2Q earnings overnight for clues on IT sector's business outlook in the second half of the year. Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo says market appears to be in profit-taking mode after sharp rise seen at end of Monday session, but says market funds still flowing into undervalued areas like financial holding companies; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hana Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  (086790.SE) +2.2% at KRW35,000, Shinhan Financial (055550.SE) +1.3 at KRW48,600. Adds, index likely to remain in 1730-1750 range for the rest of the day. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.1% at 20,454.76 midday amid caution after index's 3.1% rise over past 3 sessions, with modest trading volume at HK$26.45 billion. "As investment sentiment improves, rotational buying may emerge and investors will be watching out for underperformers that suffered from undue selling," says Taifook. Some HK property stocks outperform supported by strong home sales recently, with \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) +1.1% at HK$31.30, \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +1.1% at HK$14.72. China banks higher ahead of Agricultural Bank of China's (1288.HK) A-share debut Thursday, H-share Friday, with ICBC (1398.HK) +0.5% at HK$5.86, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) +0.6% at HK$6.52. Taifook says correction in China insurers offers bargain hunting opportunities, with China Life (2628.HK) down 0.4% at HK$33.80, adding to 3.7% loss Monday, on earnings concerns after removal of guaranteed return rate (GRR) cap of 2.5% on traditional life insurance products. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0452 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may have peaked for session at 1,215 earlier, as exporters likely to sell to cap upside should pair rise as high as that level again, says local bank trader. Adds, "I reckon pair could test below 1,210 if (more) exporters join in to sell around current levels, but any steep downside isn't likely either, given weak Chinese shares." Puts floor for day in 1,207-1,208 region. Another local bank trader notes, "support around the key 1,200 level seems to have firmed up today," but will have to see how much of foreign funds investing in local shares flow in later. Pair last up at 1,211.3 vs 1,202.0 late yesterday in Seoul, but off a high of 1,215.0. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand shares lower as market volumes remain anaemic, sentiment toward NZ results season softens, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  Institutional Broker David Price; NZX-50 last down 0.1% at 3009.63. Adds, hard to imagine outlook environment for companies that is good; "where people who were previously cautiously optimistic will now just be cautious." Says \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ) down 0.3% at NZ$5.80 after \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  made "small cuts" to 2010 earnings estimates. Says bluechip Fletcher Building down 0.1% at NZ$7.70 as people worry about residential house building sector. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese stocks may benefit in scenario new coalition is formed between ruling Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) and New Komeito party centering on policies spurring economic growth led by robust consumer spending, says Shoji Hirakawa, chief strategist at UBS Securities at Tuesday seminar in Tokyo. Notes DPJ will likely need to team with New Komeito to clinch parliamentary majority, similar policy stances between pair over tax breaks for lower-income families and child allowances/tuition waivers. "Foreign investors will turn their eyes to Japan if consumption rises," he says. Adds regarding timing, shares likely to benefit from autumn as political uncertainties lessen with coalition details expected in September. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares extend gains, rising to fresh 30-month high, underpinned by overnight gains in U.S. stocks. Bellwether PSEi closes 0.8% higher to 3435.85 amid heavy trading. "Technicals are good...this (market advance) should continue," says Joseph Roxas, Eagle Equities president. Tips index to head towards 3450 as market eyes overseas leads, next support at 3370. Leading market gain are \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH), +2.3% PHP22.50; \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH),+ 2.7% at PHP19.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1329.56 midday with banks leading gains, though index trapped within narrow 3-point range in tepid morning trade; dealers say KLCI's failure to break 1335 resistance (June high) suggests upside momentum may be fading. "If the 1335 level is not tested soon, the market may fall back to the 1300 to 1320 region," dealer says. Banks higher on expectations rising interest rates would boost profit margins. Maybank (1155.KU) +0.4% at MYR7.65, CIMB (1023.KU) +0.3% at MYR7.12; among decliners, \uc2 Scomi Marine\uc2  (7045.KU) down 3.5% at 41.5 sen, Gamuda (5398.KU) 0.9% lower at MYR3.33. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares surrender early gains by end of morning session as sentiment hit by weakness in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  bourses on concerns over more state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chinese property market. STI off 0.2% at 2920.67 midday vs +0.5% at 2938.89 earlier, expected to hold above 2900 for rest of session. Market breadth negative vs 4 gainers for every decliner initially. NRA Capital chairman Kevin Scully remains bullish despite near-term jitters, citing decent P/E valuations for global equities; "the (second-quarter) results will affirm these attractive valuations which I believe will trigger the resumption of the markets' uptrend." Adds participation should pick up with Summer holiday period nearing end; "fund managers should be back and taking stock of their investment weightings in the last two weeks of July." Notable decliners among blue chips include \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J36.SG), off 1.7% at US$39.82, OCBC (O39.SG), off 1.1% at S$8.90, NOL (N03.SG), off 1.0% at S$1.99. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +1.8% at 509.08 on bargain-hunting. "Investors have started to return to the market after the index fluctuated around 500 for 2 weeks," says analyst with Thang Long Securities. Fund manager Dragon Capital's decision yesterday not to disband $418 million investment fund, gains in U.S. stocks supporting sentiment. Volume average with 47 million shares valued at 1.38 trillion traded. Among most actives, \uc2 Saigon Thuong Tin Commercial Joint Stock Bank\uc2  (STB.VH) +2.4% at VND17,200, \uc2 Saigon Securities Inc\uc2 . (SSI.VH) +4.8% at VND34,700, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  General Services JSC (PET.VH) +4.9% at VND25,500. Thinks index likely to reach 530 in near term. (trong-khanh.vu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei turns negative in afternoon session, now down 0.3% at 9521.53 as Asian markets weaken, USD/JPY falls (now 88.44 vs 88.87 earlier, while EUR/JPY at 111.34 vs 112.07); exporters also trimming gains, notes Japanese brokerage manager. "This week's trading catalysts are short-lived," he says, citing fading excitement from \uc2 Alcoa\uc2 's (AA) strong 2Q earnings. Investor attention now shifting to Intel (INTC) as its earnings report due later in global trading day; if result cheers investors, \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO), other chip-sensitive shares may rise tomorrow. For rest of session today, Nikkei support seen at 9500. Steel shares down on profit-taking after rising sharply yesterday on strong China trade surplus data; \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  (5401.TO) off 1.9% at Y302. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting back down after ticking up as high as 0.7145 earlier in session, says David Curd, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor for Direct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "It's just sort of easing back toward the lows of the day at the moment." Pair last at 0.7118. Mood was fairly positive early, in particular after \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  kicked off U.S. earnings season on positive note; "It's been one of those risk on again stories." Weaker equity markets across much of Asia now weighing on sentiment. Tips support to hold around 0.7080, resistance at 0.7150. Says investors interested in this week's domestic data, such as retail sales, 2Q CPI but main focus on U.S. earnings. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays moderately higher midday in quiet trading after higher-than-expected dollar-yuan central parity; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7756 vs 6.7711 last close. "Some people thought perhaps there was a typo in the central parity rate (set at 6.7802 vs 6.7718 yesterday) as many expected it would be closer to the 6.7700 level," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. "It appears the recent low of CNY6.7676 could be the near-term bottom for the dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times last week to defend the 6.7700 level." Adds, market participants awaiting for macroeconomic data, including 2Q GDP, Thursday. Any signs of slowing growth could stall CNY appreciation trend for time being, he says. USD/CNY tipped at 6.7650-6.7800 for session. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 13, 2010 01:08 ET (05:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100713e67d000iv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
