\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HSI Off 0.3%; 21,000 Key Cap Near Term-Prudential\b0\par\par\uc2 1,649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2010\par 08:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.3% at 20,401.67 in modest profit-taking vs 3.1% rise over past 3 sessions, with lackluster trading given light volume at HK$28.46 billion. Prudential Associate Director Alvin Cheung suggests reentry level at around 19,600. "The local bourse is taking a breather after its latest gains, with regional markets also trading in a sluggish mood." Tips 21,000 as key cap near-term. Suggests investors to look for China banks and China consumption plays due to their positive earnings outlook. On China property stocks, house says investors should remain cautious, as recent slowdown in sale growth suggests earnings outlook turns mediocre. China Overseas (0688.HK) down 0.3% at HK$16.04, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Resources Land\f28 \uc2 \f2  (1109.HK) down 1.5% at HK$15.62. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stock market remains quiet as investors retreat to sidelines after pocketing gains from early advance, wary of taking new positions amid renewed concerns of more government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's property market. Overall volume modest at under 700 million shares worth S$627.8 million vs yesterday's 1.02 billion worth S$1.10 billion. STI off 0.2% at 2920.80, with support at 2900. "We expect the STI to end lower today, owing to the lack of clear direction and catalysts in the broader market," says CIMB. Market breadth remains negative. Top 10 most active stocks comprise good mix of small, mid, large caps, including \uc2 Thakral Corp\uc2 . (T04.SG), flat at S$0.03, Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 0.9% at S$1.16, \uc2 Asiasons Capital\uc2  (5ET.SG), +5.0% at S$0.21, Bund Centre Investment (LJ1.SG), +3.0% at S$0.515, Macquarie International Infrastructure Fund (M41.STG), +5.1% at S$0.52. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] The markets will watch Tuesday's Greek T-bill auction closely as uncertainty surrounding the sovereign crisis remains high, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  strategist, Cagdas Aksu. EUR1.26 billion 26-week T-bills are being auctioned which is expected to pass without any problems. "We expect the auction to be well bid, with demand coming predominantly from domestic banks, as is usual for Greek T-bill auctions," he says. "However, we do not consider today's auction a significant test of market confidence in Greece or a sign that the country could return easily to the capital markets, basically because of the sharp difference between the T-bills and the GGB market, where international banks have a major role." Greek/German bond yield spreads have tightened in the last two weeks with the 2-year Greek/German spread 16 bps tighter at +920 bps, 5-years 7.5 bps tighter at +950 bps, 10-years 6.5 bps tighter at +775 bps and 30-year spreads 12 bps tighter at +599.5 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1329.62 with market mixed in thin volume of 285 million shares. Local dealer fears market may slip into red later on lack of buying momentum, absence of fresh leads; notes breach of next resistance at 1335 critical for index to rise further. Market breadth tad negative with 285 decliners vs 247 advancers; decliners include Scomi (7158.KU) down 1.3% at MYR0.395, Gamuda (5398.KU) down 0.9% at MYR3.33, HLG Capital (5274.KU) down 4.9% at MYR1.16. Advancers include Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) +1.5% at MYR2.70, Maybank (1155.KU) +0.5% at MYR7.66. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] Citi estimates Poland consumer price inflation slowing to 2.1% year on year in June from 2.2% in May, but adds the biggest uncertainty surrounds the scale of food price changes, mainly because of unfavorable weather conditions in May and June, which might have affected fruit and vegetable prices. "Demand side factors probably supported further disinflation and we estimate the core inflation (excluding food and energy) fell to 1.4% in June," Citi says. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] China real-estate prices are falling, especially first-tier cities like Beijing, but not collapsing, says Standard Chartered analyst Feng Zhi-wei. She says most Chinese developers have liquidity cushion, diversified land bank that can withstand tighter credit lines; recommends to stock up Country Garden (2007.HK) 2017 bond, Hopson (0754.HK) 2012 bond, Evergrande (3333.HK) 2015 bonds. "Looking ahead, we believe that the Chinese property market will continue to be policy-driven and from the developers' viewpoint, managing liquidity will be key." Adds, large developers with sizable land bank may offer "meaningful" discounts in 2H to boost sales. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are a tad lower Tuesday as European equities are set to open slightly higher. The market will be keeping a close eye on a EUR1.5Bln Greek T-bill sale later in the session and the ZEW indicator of economic sentiment due at 0900 GMT. "We forecast sufficient demand since the Greek financial agency is likely to ensure sufficient loyalty ahead of the auction, particularly on the part of domestic banks," WestLB says, adding that the coupon is likely to be around 5%. The Netherlands also offers bonds for up to EUR3Bln. September bunds down 0.02 at 129.32 and the 10-year bund is slightly lower at 103.55 to yield 2.591%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Region's stock markets mostly lower, dragged by Shanghai as investors in China have their hopes for loosening of property cooling measures dashed; Shanghai Composite down 1.8%. Nikkei down 0.1%, S&P/ASX 200 off 0.7%, Kospi +0.1%, HSI down 0.1%, STI flat, Taiex down 0.6%, Sensex down 0.2%. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in tight ranges, JPY benefiting slightly from safe-haven bid as stocks slump; EUR/USD at 1.2566 vs 1.2593 in late NY trade, EUR/JPY at 111.30 vs 111.55, USD/JPY at 88.54 vs 88.59. Australian business confidence little changed in June after substantial fall in May; June NAB business confidence index down 1 point on-month at 4 points; business conditions index rose 2 points to 8 points. May value of Aussie personal finance +0.1% on month seasonally adjusted at A$6.79 billion. Japan May revised industrial production +0.1% on month vs down 0.1% preliminary print. IMF Managing Director Dominque Strauss-Kahn says IMF working closely with Asia through South Korea on developing global financial safety net, which agency should be able to deliver at Seoul G-20 meeting in November. Asian Development Bank could soon raise economic growth forecasts for Asian countries, its president Haruhiko Kuroda says; adds, Asian economies' 1H growth much faster than expected, with some showing signs inflationary pressures increasing. Philippine May exports +37% on year at $4.24 billion, +18% on month. NZ June food prices +1.3% on month; but down 2.0% on-year. Spot gold at $1,199.50/oz, up $2.40 vs NY close. August Nymex crude oil futures down 32 cents at $74.63/bbl. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares nearly flat midday, +0.04% at 819.90, after +0.2% at 821.50 earlier, in lackluster session. Market largely dominated by retail players while many foreign participants sidelined, awaiting fresh factors. Eyes on BOT rate decision tomorrow, with most economists expecting a 25 bps hike. "After recent steep gains, I don't see any supportive factors for further rises in the near-term and our recommendation is for investors to sell on strength," says Bualuang Securities strategist Pongrat Ratanatawanananda. Resistance for day at 830 while support pegged at 816. \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +1.85 at THB84.25. TMB Bank (TMB.TH) down 1.15 at THB1.74, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) flat at THB94.25, \uc2 Thai Airways International\uc2  (THAI.TH) off 2.3% at THB31.25.(phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0617 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude finds support around $74.50/bbl after selloff triggered by weak EUR. Trade relatively thin with no clear direction for market, Daiichi Shohin analyst Koichi Murakami says; adds, prices likely to stay around current levels until tomorrow when U.S. retail sales, EIA weekly oil data due. August Nymex crude down 39 cents at $74.56/bbl. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects Poland's June inflation to remain flat vs May at 2.2% on the year. "The inflation is currently within the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target of 2.5%, give or take one percentage point and it should remain in this territory for the rest of 2010," Danske adds. But it notes economic activity in Poland is progressing at high speed and this could translate into upward pressure on inflation. "We forecast inflation at 2.6% on the year this year and 3% next year," Danske adds. Poland June CPI data will be released Tuesday at 1200 GMT. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are down 0.12 at 129.22 in early trade, retracing some of the gains made on Monday and WestLB sees immediate support at 129.16, followed by 129.08 and then at 128.76. To the upside, resistance is pegged at 129.57, 129.70 and 129.86. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) [ 07-13-10 0254ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100713e67d0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
