 he says, citing fading excitement from \uc2 Alcoa\uc2 's (AA) strong 2Q earnings. Investor attention now shifting to Intel (INTC) as its earnings report due later in global trading day; if result cheers investors, \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO), other chip-sensitive shares may rise tomorrow. For rest of session today, Nikkei support seen at 9500. Steel shares down on profit-taking after rising sharply yesterday on strong China trade surplus data; \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  (5401.TO) off 1.9% at Y302. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting back down after ticking up as high as 0.7145 earlier in session, says David Curd, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor for Direct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "It's just sort of easing back toward the lows of the day at the moment." Pair last at 0.7118. Mood was fairly positive early, in particular after \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  kicked off U.S. earnings season on positive note; "It's been one of those risk on again stories." Weaker equity markets across much of Asia now weighing on sentiment. Tips support to hold around 0.7080, resistance at 0.7150. Says investors interested in this week's domestic data, such as retail sales, 2Q CPI but main focus on U.S. earnings. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays moderately higher midday in quiet trading after higher-than-expected dollar-yuan central parity; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7756 vs 6.7711 last close. "Some people thought perhaps there was a typo in the central parity rate (set at 6.7802 vs 6.7718 yesterday) as many expected it would be closer to the 6.7700 level," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. "It appears the recent low of CNY6.7676 could be the near-term bottom for the dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times last week to defend the 6.7700 level." Adds, market participants awaiting for macroeconomic data, including 2Q GDP, Thursday. Any signs of slowing growth could stall CNY appreciation trend for time being, he says. USD/CNY tipped at 6.7650-6.7800 for session. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0230 GMT misstated the index rise in the headline. The correct version follows:) Kospi trims earlier gains, now +0.2% at 1738.21 in thin trade, after Chinese stocks open lower. "Investors are not making aggressive moves today...the market is waiting to check Intel's results tonight," says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 ' Chung Myoung-ji. Adds Kospi may break strong 1750 resistance if Intel delivers a brighter outlook. Auto makers leading decliners on profit taking after recent gains, with \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.8% at KRW138,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 1.7% at KRW32,400. Meanwhile, \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) down 4.1% at KRW13,900 after news that deadline for binding bids to buy carmaker has been extended by 3 weeks as some of 6 bidders sought more time for due diligence on Ssangyong. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD tad down at 32.123 midday in thin trade vs 32.250 late yesterday in quiet trade, though slightly higher vs around 32.100 before usual suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted pair, traders say; weak stocks (off 0.5%) and firmer USD/KRW weigh on local pair. "U.S. dollar bulls are few, while U.S. dollar bears don't dare to short" because of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says trader at government-owned bank. Adds, KRW looks weaker today so players won't short USD in afternoon, tips pair in tight 32.090-32.150 band afternoon, and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely again to push pair higher in late session. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com) [ 07-13-10 0052ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000