\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Up A Tad; Demand Rises On Euro's Rebound Vs Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2010\par 10:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.7790               6.7733 \par USD/CNY OTC 0730 GMT     6.7749    -0.06%     6.7758 \par              High        6.7802    +0.02% \par              Low         6.7747    -0.06% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan was slightly higher against the U.S. dollar Tuesday afternoon, driven by demand for the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  following the euro's rebound against the dollar during Asian trading hours.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.7749 around 0730 GMT, down from Monday's close of CNY6.7758. It traded between CNY6.7747 and CNY6.7802.\par \par \uc2 The yuan was weaker against the dollar in the morning and early afternoon, after the People's Bank of China set the session's dollar-yuan central parity rate higher for the second consecutive day at 6.7790, up from 6.7730 Monday and a record low of 6.7720 Friday.\par \par \uc2 The daily dollar-yuan fixing is the mid-point around which the spot rate is allowed to trade up or down by as much as 0.5% intraday.\par \par \uc2 The fixing level was higher than around 6.7740 some traders had anticipated, and indicates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely intends to steer the dollar higher against the yuan, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "It signals the PBOC intends to control the speed of yuan appreciation," a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank said. "It seems the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to control the dollar-yuan around CNY6.7700-CNY6.7800 in the near term."\par \par \uc2 The yuan had appreciated 0.74% against the dollar as of Monday since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on June 19 a decision to drop its nearly two-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar and promised to make the yuan exchange rate more flexible.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened Tuesday but reversed losses in the afternoon, when the euro rose against the dollar after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator reiterated Europe remains a key investment market for China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's biggest.\par \par \uc2 Around 0730 GMT, the euro was at $1.2585, up from $1.2541 in late New York trade Monday.\par \par \uc2 However, traders said most market participants were cautious, because \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the dollar if it falls too quickly against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 They pointed to the dramatic late-session market turnabout Monday, when the dollar rose to CNY6.7758 from a session low of CNY6.7676 within the last hour of trading, as signs of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards rose a tad to 6.6718/6.6768 from 6.6710/6.6760 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 -By Joy C. Shaw, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; joy.shaw@dowjones.com [ 06-07-10 0824GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100706e676000nu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
