\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-Malaysia c.bank-no need for change after yuan move\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2010\par 04:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2  * No plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopts yuan flexibility\par \par \uc2  * Malaysia exports won't suffer from stronger ringgit\par \par \uc2  * Monetary policy to support recovery, remove imbalances  (Repeats item first carried late on Wednesday)\par \par \uc2  By Gilbert Kreijger\par \par \uc2  AMSTERDAM, June 23 (Reuters) - Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Wednesday she did not expect to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets following \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement of a more flexible policy on the yuan.\par \par \uc2  China said over the weekend it would allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  greater flexibility, strengthening Malaysia's ringgit <MYR=> versus the dollar, but Bank Negara Malaysia Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz said that Malaysia would stick to its exchange rate policy and saw limited impact in Asia.\par \par \uc2  "This is not expected to have implications on the region in terms of impact on financial markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, in the region," Zeti told Reuters.\par \par \uc2  "We will continue with our exchange rate regime that we have in place. It has served us very well," said Zeti, who was visiting the Netherlands en route to the Bank for International Settlements' annual meeting in Switzerland later this week.\par \par \uc2  Zeti said the ringgit's appreciation over the past year -- it is up almost 9 percent to trade at 3.231 to the dollar -- reflects an improvement of Malaysia's economy, which grew by 10.1 percent in the first quarter, the fastest pace in a decade. [ID:nSGE64C0DV]\par \par \uc2  "We will promote orderly market conditions. Therefore -- for the most time of course -- it does not involve any \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," she added.\par \par \uc2  Zeti said euro zone debt woes had not posed a threat to market stability and only when there was a potential disruption or disorderly market conditions would Bank Negara consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2  "We were there to stabilise the market condition during that period -- during Lehman's fall and the period that followed. That was to prevent depreciation," Zeti said.\par \par \uc2  GLOBAL GROWTH UNCERTAINTY    She said, however, euro zone debt problems and developments in the United States were creating uncertainty for global economic growth.\par \par \uc2  Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hiked interest rates <MYINTR=ECI> at two consecutive meetings this year, each time by 25 basis points so that its key rate now stands at 2.5 percent, and has repeatedly said that rates must "normalise".\par \par \uc2  "There are risks to the external conditions and we do have to take that into account in determining the extent to which further normalisation will take place," Zeti said.\par \par \uc2  "We've also said that for the rest of this year monetary policy will remain supportive of the economy and therefore we would not be moving into a tightening phase. This is because we assess inflation will remain modest during this year."\par \par \uc2  A low interest rate could push money into "informal sectors" or high-risk activities or create asset bubbles that may hurt the economy when conditions change, Zeti said.\par \par \uc2  "The process of normalisation is aimed at removing the risk of a buildup of financial imbalances. We believe such financial imbalances could result in a risk to our growth."\par \par \uc2  At the same time, Malaysian exports are not expected to suffer from a stronger ringgit because of their focus on competitiveness, innovation and productivity, Zeti said.\par \par \uc2  "Our export sector has also been able to adjust to manage their position better. We have also removed many rules that give them greater flexibility in managing their foreign financial transactions." she said.\par \par \uc2  Zeti said the government, which had a 2009 budget deficit of 7 percent of gross domestic product, would have the opportunity to start cutting subsidies on food, petrol, electricity and gas, as it announced last month.\par \par \uc2  "The government can take time but it is now that we have achieved stable economic conditions it is good to start the process," she added.  (Editing by Stephen Nisbet)  \par \par \uc2 MALAYSIA-ECONOMY/ (INTERVIEW, REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100624e66o0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
