\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Flat As Spot Rangebound\b0\par\par\uc2 1,559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2010\par 04:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0247 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month at-the-money USD/JPY options flat vs NY Wednesday at 10.65%/11.35% as spot rangebound, says options dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Says actual volatility now around 8.5%, meaning implied volatilities can fall to 9.0% or lower if USD/JPY spot fails to move more than Y1 a day. "We need at least one- or two-yen move a day to prompt investors to start buying contracts," he says. Earlier, one investor spotted selling 3-week USD-call/JPY-put options with 90 strike, $50 million face value at 11.15% while others sold 1-week USD-put/JPY-call with 89 strike, $100 million face value at 10.75%. Deals suggest investors bet USD/JPY to trade around 89.00-90.00 range for weeks to come. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.2% at 20,815.85 with no strong lead from regional markets to help affirm clear direction though Fed's decision to keep rates at low levels should be positive for equity markets ahead, says Linus Yip, strategist at First Shanghai. HK property stocks mostly outperforming vs broader market after Fed keeps rates unchanged, as HK banks tend to follow rate moves in U.S. due to local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to USD. Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +0.5% at HK$94.30, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +0.5% at HK$113.00. "The outlook on local property market remains positive given the low interest rate environment, while a gradual China yuan appreciation may raise purchasing power of mainland residential buyers of Hong Kong apartments." Expects index to stay in 20,500-21,000 range near-term. Cnooc (0883.HK) down 0.7% at HK$13.68 after crude price weakness. Volume modest at HK$9.45 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Korea unlikely to raise policy rate by big margin despite stronger-than-expected growth forecasts for this year, says KTB Securities economist Lee Seung-soo; "the low base for last year's growth plays a part in the government's bullish GDP projections for this year. Besides, inflation won't be as serious as some fear because the government is determined to minimize any increase in public service charges and corporate demand for money is still low." Adds, 25-bp rate hike possible in August or September to give room to maneuver in policy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but expects maximum 50-bp rise for whole of this year. South Korea's GDP likely to expand 5.8% this year vs previous forecast of 5.0%, according to finance ministry announcement earlier in day. Inflation expected to average 2.9% this year, around 3.0% next year--well within BOK's target band of 2.0%-4.0%. (in-soo.nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] One big U.S. fund in past month buying $5 billion worth of 3-year to 5-year of USD-call/JPY-put options with strike around 120, say several senior dealers in Japan. One says that fund buys $200 million to $500 million each day. Fund probably expects USD/JPY to rise to around 120 in coming years as Fed by that time should resume hiking rates while Japan economic fundamentals remain weak. Adds price of these options still cheap, meaning "if these option deals fit their trading direction, then it's a good purchase." Expects that fund's buying of such options will increase ahead. Big amount of purchased options mean USD/JPY trade may be affected heavily if spot indeed trades around 120 in next few years. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan May core CPI likely down 1.3% on year vs 1.5% drop in April, marking 15th-straight month of decline, according to poll of economists by Dow Jones, Nikkei; such result would suggest recovery in broader economy has started to help pick up domestic demand, prevent prices from decreasing further. But concerns linger that deflation could weigh on recovery, putting more pressure on BOJ to take further steps as government now trying to make consumer prices turn positive in fiscal year starting April 2011. June core CPI for Tokyo area, leading indicator of nationwide price trend, likely down 1.4% on year vs 1.6% fall in May, those economists say. Data due 2330 GMT Thursday, Friday morning in Japan. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.2% at 9940.37 midday after opening lower, largely on low-volume, defensive-oriented pharmaceutical share buying, led by Eisai (4523.TO), up 1.9% at Y3,035. Stronger yen on U.S. Fed's dim outlook, stand-pat on rates hit shares early, but sense of relief also pervasive as USD/JPY hovered at upper 89 level (without falling much further, now 89.94). "There are also some positive domestic factors like signs of consumption tax hike and fiscal restructuring" providing support, says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. For afternoon session, sees trading narrow range between 9850-9950. Real estate stocks also outperforming, with \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) up 1.9% at 1,656. Softbank (9984.TO) up 1.7% at Y2,477 as iPhone 4 sales start from today. Meanwhile, Toyota (7203.TO) down 0.6% at Y3,200 as China strike concerns weigh. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil falls in Asia this morning, sentiment among analysts remains pessimistic on weak U.S. housing sales data, disappointing inventories; signs point to bearish demand outlook. "People were buying commodities as they were banking on a recovering U.S. economy (which would support oil demand)...but not anymore," says Ryuichi Sato, analyst with Mizuho Securities; adds, oil may fall to $73/bbl-$75/bbl. "The odds are for sentiment to remain a bit gloomy and prices will likely remain depressed. There could be a bit more room to go to the downside," says Mike Sander with Sander Capital Advisors. Nymex August crude contract down 7 cents at $76.28/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher, helped by apparent tightness in USD supply; pair last quoted at 46.08 in moderate volume, up from 46.05 late yesterday and today's early low 45.935, but off earlier 46.12 high. Support pegged at 45.80, resistance 46.30. "The dollar opened weak but there appears to be a short squeeze in the market after Monday's strong peso move following China's yuan policy announcement fizzled. Supply also isn't helped by slowing remittance," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares likely flat today following mixed movement in other regional bourses, dealers say. "We expect the main index to move between 2898-2950 today," says Budhi Ruseno, a director at Bhakti Sekuritas; main index ended down 0.3% at 2924.79 yesterday. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), \uc2 Bank Tabungan Negara\uc2  (BBTN.JK), Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK), Antam (ANTM.JK) tipped as long-term buy if share prices continue falling today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD opens lower, driven by inflows to broader Asia following FOMC's gloomier comments overnight on U.S. economy, dovishness on interest rates, says local bank trader. "Inflows to Taiwan are also particularly encouraged by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable." Pair last at 31.999 vs yesterday's close at 32.189 (after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected last-minute \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of as much as US$100 million); tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band today. Local media reports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to unveil more measures to contain sizzling property market, while keeping policy rate unchanged; pair likely to remain around 32.00 after meeting, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tipped to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds open flat ahead of result of PBOC 3-month bill auction due around 0205 GMT; Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index last at 125.46 vs 125.44. Shenzhen-based trader at asset management firm says he expects PBOC to sell 3-month bills at 1.5704%, unchanged from June 10 (PBOC didn't auction 3-month bills last week). "If the rate is unchanged, the bond market will continue to consolidate due to still-tight liquidity situation," he says. 20-year bond issued in 2003 flat at CNY98.00, with yield at 3.5958%. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to rise on better investor sentiment after some state media forecast economic growth to speed up in 2Q vs +5.83% in 1Q. Also, "sentiment is improving because most blue-chip companies are forecast to post solid results in 2Q," says trader. Adds strong net foreign buying so far this month also support for local interest. Tips key index to range in 514-517 after ending flat at 512.82 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 06-23-10 2247ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100624e66o00029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
