. Big amount of purchased options mean USD/JPY trade may be affected heavily if spot indeed trades around 120 in next few years. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan May core CPI likely down 1.3% on year vs 1.5% drop in April, marking 15th-straight month of decline, according to poll of economists by Dow Jones, Nikkei; such result would suggest recovery in broader economy has started to help pick up domestic demand, prevent prices from decreasing further. But concerns linger that deflation could weigh on recovery, putting more pressure on BOJ to take further steps as government now trying to make consumer prices turn positive in fiscal year starting April 2011. June core CPI for Tokyo area, leading indicator of nationwide price trend, likely down 1.4% on year vs 1.6% fall in May, those economists say. Data due 2330 GMT Thursday, Friday morning in Japan. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.2% at 9940.37 midday after opening lower, largely on low-volume, defensive-oriented pharmaceutical share buying, led by Eisai (4523.TO), up 1.9% at Y3,035. Stronger yen on U.S. Fed's dim outlook, stand-pat on rates hit shares early, but sense of relief also pervasive as USD/JPY hovered at upper 89 level (without falling much further, now 89.94). "There are also some positive domestic factors like signs of consumption tax hike and fiscal restructuring" providing support, says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. For afternoon session, sees trading narrow range between 9850-9950. Real estate stocks also outperforming, with \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) up 1.9% at 1,656. Softbank (9984.TO) up 1.7% at Y2,477 as iPhone 4 sales start from today. Meanwhile, Toyota (7203.TO) down 0.6% at Y3,200 as China strike concerns weigh. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil falls in Asia this morning, sentiment among analysts remains pessimistic on weak U.S. housing sales data, disappointing inventories; signs point to bearish demand outlook. "People were buying commodities as they were banking on a recovering U.S. economy (which would support oil demand)...but not anymore," says Ryuichi Sato, analyst with Mizuho Securities; adds, oil may fall to $73/bbl-$75/bbl. "The odds are for sentiment to remain a bit gloomy and prices will likely remain depressed. There could be a bit more room to go to the downside," says Mike Sander with Sander Capital Advisors. Nymex August crude contract down 7 cents at $76.28/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher, helped by apparent tightness in USD supply; pair last quoted at 46.08 in moderate volume, up from 46.05 late yesterday and today's early low 45.935, but off earlier 46.12 high. Support pegged at 45.80, resistance 46.30. "The dollar opened weak but there appears to be a short squeeze in the market after Monday's strong peso move following China's yuan policy announcement fizzled. Supply also isn't helped by slowing remittance," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares likely flat today following mixed movement in other regional bourses, dealers say. "We expect the main index to move between 2898-2950 today," says Budhi Ruseno, a director at Bhakti Sekuritas; main index ended down 0.3% at 2924.79 yesterday. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), \uc2 Bank Tabungan Negara\uc2  (BBTN.JK), Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK), Antam (ANTM.JK) tipped as long-term buy if share prices continue falling today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD opens lower, driven by inflows to broader Asia following FOMC's gloomier comments overnight on U.S. economy, dovishness on interest rates, says local bank trader. "Inflows to Taiwan are also particularly encouraged by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable." Pair last at 31.999 vs yesterday's close at 32.189 (after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected last-minute \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of as much as US$100 million); tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band today. Local media reports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to unveil more measures to contain sizzling property market, while keeping policy rate unchanged; pair likely to remain around 32.00 after meeting, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tipped to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds open flat ahead of result of PBOC 3-month bill auction due around 0205 GMT; Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index last at 125.46 vs 125.44. Shenzhen-based trader at asset management firm says he expects PBOC to sell 3-month bills at 1.5704%, unchanged from June 10 (PBOC didn't auction 3-month bills last week). "If the rate is unchanged, the bond market will continue to consolidate due to still-tight liquidity situation," he says. 20-year bond issued in 2003 flat at CNY98.00, with yield at 3.5958%. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to rise on better investor sentiment after some state media forecast economic growth to speed up in 2Q vs +5.83% in 1Q. Also, "sentiment is improving because most blue-chip companies are forecast to post solid results in 2Q," says trader. Adds strong net foreign buying so far this month also support for local interest. Tips key index to range in 514-517 after ending flat at 512.82 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares lower early in thin trade, tracking Wall Street's tepid performance overnight. Shanghai Composite Index off 0.3% at 2562.42, faces immediate support at 2550 level. "Investors are taking wait-and-see attitude and are looking for more cues regarding the yuan movements and the strength of the global economy," says Simon Wang from \uc2 Guoyuan Securities\uc2 . Among blue chips, developers and banks down, \uc2 China Vanke Co\uc2 . (000002.SZ) off 0.3% at CNY7.18, \uc2 Industrial & Commercial Bank of China\uc2  (601398.SH) off 0.2% at CNY4.23. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.1% at 1044.42.(esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] June HSI futures down 0.4% at 20,856, suggesting further consolidation likely at open, with cash market spending past 2 sessions in 268-point range after 9-session winning streak. Contract ends yesterday at 78 points premium to cash, continuing to signal near-term bullish bias. June open interest deceases 1,803 lots to 70,995; rollover may pick up ahead of current-month contract's expiry Tuesday. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 06-23-10 2240ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100624e66o00027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
