\highlight\uc2  of as much as US$100 million); tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band today. Local media reports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to unveil more measures to contain sizzling property market, while keeping policy rate unchanged; pair likely to remain around 32.00 after meeting, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tipped to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds open flat ahead of result of PBOC 3-month bill auction due around 0205 GMT; Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index last at 125.46 vs 125.44. Shenzhen-based trader at asset management firm says he expects PBOC to sell 3-month bills at 1.5704%, unchanged from June 10 (PBOC didn't auction 3-month bills last week). "If the rate is unchanged, the bond market will continue to consolidate due to still-tight liquidity situation," he says. 20-year bond issued in 2003 flat at CNY98.00, with yield at 3.5958%. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to rise on better investor sentiment after some state media forecast economic growth to speed up in 2Q vs +5.83% in 1Q. Also, "sentiment is improving because most blue-chip companies are forecast to post solid results in 2Q," says trader. Adds strong net foreign buying so far this month also support for local interest. Tips key index to range in 514-517 after ending flat at 512.82 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares lower early in thin trade, tracking Wall Street's tepid performance overnight. Shanghai Composite Index off 0.3% at 2562.42, faces immediate support at 2550 level. "Investors are taking wait-and-see attitude and are looking for more cues regarding the yuan movements and the strength of the global economy," says Simon Wang from \uc2 Guoyuan Securities\uc2 . Among blue chips, developers and banks down, \uc2 China Vanke Co\uc2 . (000002.SZ) off 0.3% at CNY7.18, \uc2 Industrial & Commercial Bank of China\uc2  (601398.SH) off 0.2% at CNY4.23. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.1% at 1044.42.(esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] June HSI futures down 0.4% at 20,856, suggesting further consolidation likely at open, with cash market spending past 2 sessions in 268-point range after 9-session winning streak. Contract ends yesterday at 78 points premium to cash, continuing to signal near-term bullish bias. June open interest deceases 1,803 lots to 70,995; rollover may pick up ahead of current-month contract's expiry Tuesday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly higher after PBOC sets dollar-yuan central parity rate nearly flat at 6.8100 vs 6.8102 yesterday, also not far from 6.8124 last close. On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.8150 vs 6.8109 at open; tipped in 6.8000-6.8300 band for session. Traders say they will closely monitor any flows likely tied to PBOC for cues. "It's hard to predict the onshore spot market directions now, as there seems to have been more \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided flows in the market," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  clearly wants to fend off yuan appreciation expectations. They want more flexibility in yuan trading but not with a one-way move." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares +0.2% to 7594.54 ahead of Taiwan-China trade pact talks concluding today in Taipei, but trading volume remains thin. "Investors are sidelined, awaiting details later today on the early-harvest list" of items to benefit first from lower tariffs, says Grand Cathay Investment Service Assistant Vice President Mars Hsu. Adds, market upside capped even if outcome better vs expected because of shrinking trading volume. U.S. Fed stood pat on rates as expected, and Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to do the same today, which will reduce volatility, he says. Tips Taiex in tight 7550-7650 band today. TSMC (2330.TW) off 0.2% to NT$62.0, while Hon Hai (2317.TW) +0.4% to NT$123.0; Asustek (2357.TW) restarts trading strongly after capital reduction, +6.9% to NT$240.50, while spun off affiliate Pegatron (4938.TW) +8.6% to NT$39.10 on debut. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely lower but in thin trade amid lack of firm cues. Heavyweight energy plays likely to fall modestly on lower oil prices, but banking stocks likely driven by optimism over 2Q earnings, may limit losses. "Given the absence of catalysts, it's difficult (for the Thai stock market) to rise but the trend remains positive," says analyst with Ayudhya Securities Adisak Phupiphathirungul; tips SET index to test year's high at 820 next week based on technical analysis. Support tipped at 800, near-term resistance at 807. SET index ended +0.3% at 806.52 yesterday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight after Fed's dovish comments overnight. But participants don't expect any sharp gains in local bonds as expectations for domestic rate hike in 3Q remains high; 3-year yield down 3 bps at 3.90%, 5-year yield down 2 bps at 4.49%, September futures +10 ticks at 110.20. Sentiment remains weak and "it won't be easy to strengthen more" from current levels, says bond manager at local bank. Tips 3-year yield in 3.88%-3.95% band for rest of day. Participants likely to closely watch government's revised economic outlook for 2010 due around 0200 GMT. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds open mixed, yields on key 10-year paper are lower, tracking U.S. Treasurys overnight, but 5-year yields up ahead of new supply, says local bank trader. Says trading isn't active, as investors refrain from taking large positions ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting scheduled later the day. 10-year bonds yield down at 1.4313% vs yesterday's close 1.4377%, 5-year bonds yield up at 1.0345% vs 1.0308%; trader says new batch of 5-year bonds will start trading on June 29, some investors are trimming their holdings of this maturity ahead of new supply. (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 06-23-10 2225ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100624e66o00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
