\par\par \b \uc2 TIMELINE - China's yuan reforms\b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2010\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, June 4 (Reuters) - In July 2005, China bowed to market and political pressure by revaluing the yuan by 2.1 percent and abandoning its decade-old peg against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In the three years following the revaluation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yuan to appreciate a further 19 percent against the dollar. The yuan's traded peak since the revaluation was 6.8099, reached on Sept. 23, 2008.\par \par \uc2 However, since July 2008, the People's Bank of China, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has effectively pegged the yuan against the dollar close to 6.83 to protect China's economy as it confronted a slowdown due to the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Pressures for China to renew the reforms, to allow the yuan to appreciate in line with its economic strength, have mounted again since late 2009 when global economies began to recover.\par \par \uc2 Here is a timetable of yuan reforms.\par \par \uc2 1988 -- China sets up semi-official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap centres around the country to allow enterprises to trade the yuan at a rate that more closely reflects market demand.\par \par \uc2 1994, Jan. 1 - China unifies its dual exchange rates by bringing the official and swap centre rates into line, officially devaluing the yuan by 33 percent overnight to 8.7 to the dollar as part of reforms to embrace a "socialist market economy".\par \par \uc2 April - China sets up its first interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 China Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System -- in Shanghai. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the yuan stable.\par \par \uc2 1996, Dec. 1 - China allows the yuan to be fully convertible under the current account.\par \par \uc2 1994-1996 -- The yuan strengthens steadily from 8.7 to the dollar to around 8.28.\par \par \uc2 1997-1999 -- China wins wide praise for keeping the yuan stable during the Asian financial crisis despite pressure to devalue. The yuan was boxed between 8.2770 and 8.2800 for about three years through frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2000 -- The yuan is allowed to close slightly outside the firm end of its 30-basis point band, which is later widened by another 10 points to 8.2760-8.2800 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 2001, December -- China joins the World Trade Organisation and pledges to adjust its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime gradually.\par \par \uc2 2003 -- International pressure begins mounting for the yuan to appreciate to help balance global trade, including China's huge trade surplus with the United States and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 2004, Dec. 8 -- Premier Wen Jiabao says that China would move gradually to a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.\par \par \uc2 2005, July 21 - China revalues the yuan by 2.1 percent and revises the rules governing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, saying it had shifted to "a managed floating exchange rate based on market supply and demand with reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". (For a FACTBOX on the system, please click) (Reporting by Lu Jianxin and Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA/YUAN (TIMELINE)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100604e664000ox\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
