\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY MARKETS-Singapore rates ease more; China yields still up\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2010\par 10:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Singapore interbank rates ease, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cash helps\par \par \uc2  * China repo rate dips, other yields stay high\par \par \uc2  * Wider dollar LIBOR-OIS spread but localised issue\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 3 (Reuters) - China's benchmark repo rate eased slightly on Thursday as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  improved onshore liquidity while Singapore's interbank rates dropped further, aided by falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards and cash injections by the authorities.\par \par \uc2  Interbank swap offered rates in Singapore <SGDDFIX=ABSG> have been falling consistently every day for the past week, taking the 3-month rate down 14 basis points to 0.40 on Thursday. These rates are implied yields derived from Singapore dollar forwards.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said that had resulted from an improvement in onshore cash availability and a move in U.S. dollar forwards, the latter possibly owing to receiving of U.S. dollar forwards by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS).\par \par \uc2  "I guess it's just the fact that the MAS has been giving out a lot of liquidity, receiving U.S. dollars, and the situation in Europe seems to be taking a breather," a Singapore trader said.\par \par \uc2  Liquidity also seemed to be ample in dollar funding markets, judging by the responses at the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's funding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for banks and the feeble demand for dollar funding at dollar swap lines the U.S. Federal Reserve has reinstated with other central banks.\par \par \uc2  Yet the dollar LIBOR-OIS spread -- the spread between interbank rates and expectations of policy yields -- continued to widen, pointing to difficulty in obtaining dollar cash at least for some players.\par \par \uc2   In Singapore, 3-month dollars <SIUSDD=ABSG> were quoted at an average 0.539 percent, continuing to trend lower bit by bit from last week's peak at 0.54667 percent.\par \par \uc2  The spread between LIBOR <LIBOR=> and overnight-indexed swaps (OIS) was 34 bps, its widest in 11 months.\par \par \uc2  Most analysts however agreed that the problem was not one of availability of cash, rather it was the solvency of a few European banks.\par \par \uc2  "European banks are facing those penalty rates because of market uncertainty about their financial condition and doubt about the ability of their respective sovereign governments to live up to their guarantee commitments," Bob Eisenbeis, Chief Monetary Economist at Cumberland Advisers wrote to clients.\par \par \uc2  In China, the benchmark 7-day repo rate dipped after steadily climbing for more than two weeks in anticipation of an improvement in the tight money markets.\par \par \uc2  Other yields rose, including that at a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction of three-month bills, as banks continued to feel the crunch from  brokerages and mutual funds setting aside money for Wednesday's 40 billion yuan convertible bond issue by Bank of China .\par \par \uc2  Those subscriptions are locked up until June 7. Moreover, there is a pipeline of equity and rights issues by other Chinese lenders.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to sell 110 billion yuan ($16 billion) of three-year bills at a lower yield of 2.68 percent compared with 2.70 percent at the last auction.\par \par \uc2  But the yield on its 5 billion yuan three-month bills rose more than 4 basis points from last week's auction to 1.53 percent, up for a third week in a row, as the PBOC has struggled to attract demand for shorter tenors. [ID:nTOE65202O]\par \par \uc2  The 7-day bond repurchase rate <CN7DRP=CFXS> fell more than 12 bps to 3.1526 percent, remaining at a high level but hinting at a gradual easing of pressure in the money market as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from conducting short-term bond repos for three weeks in a row, and has cut back on the one-year and three-month bills it has issued.\par \par \uc2  This week, it has injected a net 14 billion yuan, far lower than the 145 billion yuan last week but a sharp contrast with its aggressive draining of funds and raises in banks' required reserves in the first few months of the year.  (Reporting by Vidya Ranganathan; Editing by Jan Dahinten)    \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MONEY/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|SI|Z|RBN|J|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100603e663000mn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
