\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Up Late; Speculation Over PBOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2010\par 12:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds closing levels.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.8280               6.8279 \par USD/CNY OTC 0930 GMT     6.8291    +0.02%     6.8310 \par              High        6.8317    +0.05% \par              Low         6.8288    +0.01% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan rose against the U.S. dollar late Wednesday, reversing its earlier decline amid speculation about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it fell sharply in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.8291 around 0930 GMT, down from Tuesday's close of CNY6.8310, the highest closing level since Feb. 12 when it settled at CNY6.8330. It traded between CNY6.8288 and CNY6.8317.\par \par \uc2 Traders said they noted some dollar selling on the automatic price-matching system below market levels. The price-matching system is a little-used anonymous trading platform that dealers believe the People's Bank of China occasionally employs to signal its intentions to the market without officially showing its hand.\par \par \uc2 "If it weren't for these dollar sales, the spot level would have risen above CNY6.8320 in the morning as there was ongoing dollar demand," said a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank.\par \par \uc2 A Guangzhou-based trader at a foreign bank said: "Given the strong dollar demand from importers, it's very likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered dollar liquidity to stabilize the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Traders said the stable dollar-yuan central parity rate shows Beijing's desire to damp volatility in the yuan. The PBOC set the dollar-yuan central parity rate for the session at 6.8280, little changed from 6.8279 Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 An official at the news department of the PBOC said he wasn't aware of any market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the euro's recent weakness and rising tensions on the Korean peninsula had raised demand for the dollar on the Chinese market. The euro was trading at $1.2322 at 0945 GMT, compared with $1.2328 late Tuesday in New York.\par \par \uc2 Offshore dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards were lower in a volatile session, after bargain hunting in the yuan emerged when one-year NDFs surged above key psychological resistance of 6.8000 in the morning, driven by hedge funds' short covering of dollar positions, traders said. Around 0930 GMT, one-year dollar-yuan non-deliverable forwards were around 6.7660/6.7710, down from 6.7850/6.7950 late Tuesday and an intraday high of around 6.8060.\par \par \uc2 A Shanghai-based trader at a European bank said NDFs may resume rising in the near term if more hedge funds unwind short dollar-yuan positions they built earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 "If hedge funds are cutting their losses (on short dollar positions), the volume of dollar-yuan short covering will be quite large. One-year NDFs could rise to $7.0," the trader said, without giving a time frame.\par \par \uc2 -By Joy C. Shaw and Rose Yu, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; joy.shaw@dowjones.com [ 26-05-10 1025GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100526e65q000y8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
