\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(5/21) Falling Euro Spurs Cautious  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 02:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Bob Davis in Washington, Brian Blackstone in Frankfurt and Dinny McMahon in Beijing \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Officials in the U.S. and Europe concerned about the euro's decline are cautiously talking about a policy tool they haven't used in a decade: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Policy makers have been content to let markets set the value of the euro, which has fallen about 17% since early December, worrying U.S. exporters who face European competition and raising fear of inflation in Germany. Expectations of the prospects of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed up the euro to near $1.26, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had slipped below $1.23 earlier. Late in the day, the euro traded at around $1.25.\par \par \uc2 Marco Annunziata, chief economist at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  in London, figures the euro would have to fall to about $1.10 in a week or so to prompt policy makers to act. Such a fall could shake markets globally, boost interest rates in Europe, and threaten to undermine a global recovery.\par \par \uc2 In a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , central banks buy large amounts of a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange for a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in hopes of reversing the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. But \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  often fail.\par \par \uc2 Neither the U.S. nor the euro zone has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2000 -- and both have long been skeptical about the practice's effectiveness. The U.S. joined Europe, Japan, Britain and Canada in buying $3 billion to $5 billion of euros in September 2000 during a bout of euro weakness shortly after its introduction. Two months later, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought more euros, when the euro was trading at about 87 cents -- slightly less than half the high of about $1.60 it hit in April 2008.\par \par \uc2 "I'm really concerned about the rapid [pace] of the fall of the exchange rate," said Jean-Claude Juncker, the head of euro-zone finance ministers, on Thursday, though he said he didn't think euro's level required "immediate action."\par \par \uc2 Ted Truman, a former international economics official in the Clinton and Obama Treasury departments, said that "it's right for authorities to be thinking about possibly protesting" the fall in the euro via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Though the U.S. and Europe have spent extraordinary sums to fight the global recession, and drastically eased monetary policy, they have left \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets alone, figuring that the ups and downs of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect fundamental economics.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Board Gov. Daniel Tarullo suggested the Fed wasn't pressing for an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We don't have other action under consideration" beyond the dollar swap lines to European banks that the Fed revived earlier this month, he said at a congressional hearing Thursday. Problems in Europe could become "another source of risk to the U.S. recovery," he testified.\par \par \uc2 Many economists believe the euro is trading at an appropriate level, given Europe's troubled budget picture and diminished growth prospects. The <euro>110 billion ($136 billion) Greek loan package, followed by a nearly $1 trillion loan commitment for other troubled European countries, underscored the region's financial frailty and raised the possibility that the ECB could print money and devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . European wrangling over how tightly to regulate financial markets has also put downward pressure on the euro.\par \par \uc2 Some European officials say they believe a gradual fall in the euro is an economic plus because it will make European exports less expensive.\par \par \uc2 A decision to act can take many forms, with the simplest being statements by influential officials warning, in coded language, that they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In June 2008, Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke warned about the perils of a weak dollar. That gave the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a brief lift, but the Fed never actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A sustained turnaround didn't occur until more than a month later, when investors started flocking into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in search of havens.\par \par \uc2 Another possibility is a coordinated statement by a group of influential countries, as occurred in September 2000.\par \par \uc2 The goal of such statements is to produce the desired outcome without taking the chance on an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could backfire. One risk of acting: Investors might believe policy makers have misdiagnosed why \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising and falling or don't have the will to deal with an underlying problem. That, in turn, could lead to even-more-jarring market moves.\par \par \uc2 A big wild card is the role of China, with its $2.5 trillion in reserves. The Chinese have long said they want to diversify their funds, which are heavily weighted to the dollar, by increasing the share of euros.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say there is a chance that China could be persuaded to participate in a globally coordinated effort to prop up the euro if other nations can convince Beijing the move is in its interests.\par \par \uc2 Harvard University economist Jeffrey Frankel said Chinese participation would give a powerful boost to any action. "If you \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  now [without \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 ], markets would probably call the bluff and governments would lose," he said. "If we had the Chinese on our side, that $2.5 trillion in reserves could be intimidating, even to markets."\par \par \uc2 But it is unlikely Beijing would join in. \uc2 China Investment Corp\uc2 ., China's $300 billion sovereign-wealth fund, didn't buy Greek bonds earlier this year, which would have helped to ease the country's problems. Jesse Wang, CIC's executive vice president, said responsibility for a bailout lay with the European Union.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jon Hilsenrath in Washington contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 20, 2010 20:17 ET (00:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100521e65l0006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
