\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian Governments Ahead Of Issuance Schedule\b0\par\par\uc2 1,062 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2010\par 18:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1616 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian governments (excluding China and India) are having little trouble raising required funds on their local debt markets, \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  says, with around 41% of expected issuance for this year already completed. Indonesia and Taiwan lead the pack with 50% already done while South Korea and Taiwan are just behind. At the same time last year, these governments had only 9% of their plans completed. The bonds are getting bought up quickly as money flows into emerging-market bond funds and many countries reduced issuance size this year compared with last. China and India, \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  notes, generally start their issuance later in the year. (kejal.vyas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1609 GMT [Dow Jones] The "disparity" between LatAm and CEEMA growth and interest rates favors Latin America, says Flavia Cattan-Naslausky of RBS. LatAm will benefit from rebound in US and Asia, while CEEMA can be hurt by euro zone woes. LatAm central banks are turning hawkish, which will lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains. In Emerging Europe, rates will be lower for longer. RBS recommends betting on a basket of BRL, MXN and CLP, funded with a basket selling HUF, PLN and CZK. Alternatively, buy a USD-denominated basket option equally weighted between BRL and MXN against an equally weighted basket of HUF, PLN, CZK. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1604 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors are looking past the Greek bailout and focusing on more concerning issues. "The sharks are coming for the euro," the Pragmatic Capitalist says. "They see a wounded animal and the market is beginning to realize that there is no good long-term fix besides a substantially weaker and/or potentially dead euro." The uncertainty that could ensue in the near-term is "too great for me to fathom," blog says. "The EU has gone 'all-in' with a very poor hand. The market will eventually call their bluff and when it does it will not be pretty." (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://pragcap.com/a-few-thoughts-on-this-mornings-markets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://pragcap.com/a-few-thoughts-on-this-mornings-markets}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 1541 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil's real resumes weakening against the dollar as investors drop local assets on fears of more fallout from a fiscal crisis in Europe. At 1540 GMT the real is trading at BRL1.8100 to the dollar after ending at BRL1.7765 to the dollar Thursday. (gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1549 GMT [Dow Jones] Capital Economics alters its view of the level of the euro at the end of the year, cutting it to $1.10, from the previous forecast of $1.20. Says while some of the forces bearing down on the euro over recent months may now be easing, there are plenty of other reasons to expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall further. Also expects the euro to fall to parity by the end of 2011, as euro zone GDP growth will lag well behind that in the US, while worries about the fiscal crisis and future of the euro should persist. EUR/USD now at $1.2380. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1540 GMT [Dow Jones] "Inflation is clawing its way back in EM," says Philip Poole of \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , citing rising food and energy prices, tightening capacity constraints, failure to sterilise \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and real estate prices in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He says Asia and Brazil are most at risk, and "monetary policy in most of EM is now biased to tightening." The way to trade this trend is in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities, rather than debt, says Poole. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1530 GMT [Dow Jones] Argentina stocks and bonds are tumbling on fears over Europe's ability to ease its debt burdens and as investors seek safer assets. Argentina's Merval index of leading shares is down over 3%, while the dollar-denominated boden 2015 bond is down 1.37% in price terms at ARS312.25 ($80.11), bringing the yield to 12.81%. The government wants to issue $1 billion in similar bonds, but is likely to delay the sale if investors ask for over 10% interest. (shane.romig@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1527 GMT [Dow Jones] The former president of Venezuela's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ruth de Krivoy, said in a research note for Global Source that recent remarks by President Chavez hint at "stronger conditions for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions by brokerage firms or, in the extreme, their exclusion from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." She says the answer will be in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upcoming regulations. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1526 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR has staggered to a new session low of 1.2358 vs USD as it gets pummelled by the same risk aversion that has sent US stock indexes tumbling, with DJIA recently down about 200 points. Next big landmark for EUR/USD is the Oct '08 low of 1.2329, according to EBS via CQG. EUR has since recovered slightly to 1.2381. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1526 GMT [Dow Jones] Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also under pressure as the flight from risk intensifies, with USD reaching a session high of 1.0356 vs CAD and AUD/USD hitting a daily low at 0.8878 before rebounding slightly. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1520 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian government bonds ease further on a stock market slump and a weakening HUF to EUR, a trader says. Bond yields climb 10bp across the curve. The 3-year benchmark bond yield is 6.13%, 5-year is 6.50%, 10-year is 7.00%. Bonds will likely trade horizontally Monday as stock markets and the HUF are still undecided which direction to take after a general plunge Friday. Although markets still price a 25bp rate cut for the end-May meeting, analysts are divided whether the MPC will slash the key rate amid uncertainties and fear of the Greek contagion. EUR/HUF is 276.3 from 272.7 in the morning. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1513 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR continues to tumble vs USD and has hit a new session low at 1.2365, its lowest level since Oct 2008, according to EBS via CQG. That marked the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lowest point during the global financial crisis. (don.curren@dowjones.com) [ 05-14-10 1216ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100514e65e000b1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
