\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Refreshing Proposal On China Ppty Taxes -WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 1,307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2010\par 11:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0950 GMT [Dow Jones] China's top state think tank Wednesday urged adoption of property-ownership tax -- its own vision of Western-style property tax regime that's broadly similar to what other observers have recommended -- to put lid on run-away speculation in China's property market. But perhaps more interesting was its call to cut or eliminate several of China's property transaction taxes, which it argued have in many cases had opposite of their intended effect. Proposal for cuts in transaction taxes is refreshing, as it demonstrates some creative thinking by China's top policy wonks, and also an awareness, not often seem among China's legion of economic planners, of the unintended consequences of some market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . See "=WSJ BLOG/\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Real Time: Counterintuitive Proposal On Property Taxes" (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0948 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares trade higher with gains led by oil stocks. Benchmark KSE-100 index +0.8% at 10,555.94. "The index rose sharply as sentiment was boosted after the US government disbursed $468 million to Pakistan as compensation against the costs incurred on battling militancy," says local dealer. Adds, funds will help improve country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and might even help stabilize domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Oil and Gas Development +3.0% at PKR138.55, \uc2 Pakistan State Oil\uc2  +0.2% at PKR314.80. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR extends gains to 9,115 vs 9,100 earlier, 9,020 late yesterday, supported by news Finance Minister Mulyani quits government post to take up World Bank job. "Indonesian markets were hit hard by the news of Finance Minister Sri Mulyani's appointment to the World Bank position. Given her reputation as a reformer, the market has interpreted this development as a negative for Indonesia," says Stanchart in report. Bank Indonesia suspected of selling $100 million earlier to stem pair's rise; dealers say pair likely to remain choppy tomorrow, tips 9,080-9,150 range Thursday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0946 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR rises to 84.17 late from 84.10 Tuesday on higher demand from importers, oil related payments. "The dollar surged on renewed buying from banks to make some oil-related payments," says dealer. Notes, bank arranged around $25 million for oil payments. Traders expect dollar may weaken due to inflows related to $468 million compensation received from U.S. government to fight militancy. Dealer tips pair in 84.10-84.25 range Thursday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] It's raining orders for \uc2 HCL Technologies\uc2  (532281.BY), with India's fourth-largest IT firm by revenue inking four deals in a week. Winning streak slowly reviving hopes that the good old times are returning. But are they? Biggest of the contracts was Tuesday's five-year outsourcing order worth $500 million with U.S. drug maker Merck & Co. -- one of largest contracts HCL, Indian IT sector have seen in a while. HCL's stock was up 0.9% by early afternoon, even as Sensex, down 0.8%, was getting battered. IT sector definitely bouncing back after terrible year, judging by robust quarterly earnings reported by top software exporters. But analysts say IT firms shouldn't yet expect a repeat of heady days of billion-dollar deals, multi-year contracts; though IT spending is picking up, clients are in no hurry to shell out huge amounts of money. Industry watchers say it is now in an era of million-dollar contracts that will generally stay on lower side of $500 million mark. See "=WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: Are Billion-Dollar Deals For IT Firms On The Horizon?" (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] The market's poor reaction to the news of the bailout package for Greece has highlighted the risks of contagion, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . There are significant differences between the Greek situation and that of other peripherals and it would be simplistic to group all them together. "But while less acute, there are chronic problems in other economies such as Portugal and Spain," says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , "and markets do not appear minded to discriminate at present. The authorities need to move quickly ahead of the game." (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says it's receiving an increasing amount of client inquiries concerning the ECB buying sovereign bonds. The bank points out that ECB law does not allow the direct purchase of bonds from sovereign debt agencies, but it can operate via the secondary market. However BNP says if the ECB buys sovereign debt, the euro will enter free fall. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] The details in Wednesday's release of an uptick in the Norwegian unemployment rate dampens the bad first impression, says Handelsbanken. "In sum, however, still a negative figure, backing our forecast of unchanged rate today at 1400 CET." EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  trades at 7.8396. (karl.bruze@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] There are signs the SNB continues to be active on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. "The EUR/CHF is pretty stable at 1.43, and that would be hardly possible without quite substantial SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kasper Kirkegaard says. Such action isn't sustainable with SNB, in all probability, increasingly worried about the rationale of supporting the economy while Swiss fundamentals continue to improve, he says. The SNB declines to comment. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] The morning's data run has had little to no affect on the major \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs. From the euro zone the April Services PMI printed a 30 month high at 55.6 but was in line with the 55.5 flash estimate. Euro zone March retail sales were unchanged on the month against expectations of a small plus. From the UK the April construction PMI printed a 30 month high of 58.2. On the spots EUR/USD has held above Asia's one year low of 1.2935 and currently trades unchanged on the session at 1.2980 while GBP/USD is a tad higher at 1.5160. USD/JPY remains in the middle of its 94.50-95.00 trading band. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] The 85% mortgage lending caps proposed by the Swedish watchdog is at the high end of the 75% to 90% in the original proposal presented last year, says SEB. "The Riksbank has signaled that the FSA regulation will not be enough to control lending and that the housing market is a factor in an early rate hike." Adds that the proposal will most likely have some impact on the housing market. Sweden's two-year bond yield trades four basis points lower at 1.41% and the 10-year bond yield trades four basis points lower at 2.91%. (karl.bruze@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan April CPI +1.34% on-year vs revised +1.26% in March, marking 4th straight month of increase; government says growth boosted by increase in oil prices amid global economic recovery, with transportation prices +4.46% on-year. Results in-line with 1.5% growth forecasted by 11 economists in Dow Jones poll. Industrial Bank of Taiwan economist Yi Chia says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep key interest rate steady in near term as inflation remains mild; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may follow the monetary policy in U.S. as the island's consumer prices is still manageable," he says. April WPI +9.09% on year. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 05-05-10 0550ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100505e6550006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
