\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Will South Korea Take Bolder Steps On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Min-Jeong Lee  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2010\par 05:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The recent appointment of several key economic officials has players expecting a more forceful government presence in South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market--but a closer look at the country's fundamentals suggests their concerns may be misplaced.\par \par \uc2 With the appointment of former Vice Finance Minister Choi Joong-kyung as President Lee Myung-bak's economic adviser, market players expect the authorities to meddle in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more frequently, particularly with data suggesting the won will strengthen for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 Many traders recall aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb won strength and maintain the country's export competitiveness when Choi was a mastermind of the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in 2004 and 2008.\par \par \uc2 But the preference for a weaker won, shared by Choi and former Finance Minister Kang Man-soo, contributed to a spike in imported inflation in 2008, and the pair eventually stepped down. Kang returned last year as chief of a presidential council promoting the nation's economic competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Choi and Kang "support policies that let domestic products have more price competitiveness abroad via high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," one senior trader at a foreign bank in Seoul said. Because Choi "is such a supporter of a high exchange rate, \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to take a step toward being stronger."\par \par \uc2 Choi wasn't available for comment. Kang declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players expect to see stepped-up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the dollar approaches the psychologically important KRW1,100 level: If that's breached, they say, the won could sprint higher. In the past week or so, market players say they've spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on three different days around KRW1,107-KRW1,108.\par \par \uc2 Neither Choi nor Kang is now directly responsible for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --that's up to the Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --but the market expects the two policymakers to push for a weaker won to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 They may face an uphill battle: Several market participants said won strength is due to fundamentals that authorities can hardly fight. For example, the expected appreciation of the Chinese yuan against the dollar could drag other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar-selling interest is explosive amid robust sentiment for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general," one local bank trader said. "I don't think (the authorities) can do much to stop this."\par \par \uc2 Fund inflows to the local stock and bond markets also should keep won demand solid, especially after Moody's Investors Service upgraded Korea's sovereign debt ratings earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, as one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said, Choi may return to office a chastened man.\par \par \uc2 "Choi had an experience that he aggressively pushed for a weaker won and faced strong headwinds in the market," the BOK official said. "He may have changed now. I don't think he'll go against the market."\par \par \uc2 Given the current global context--with U.S. authorities leading a push to make \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to face criticism.\par \par \uc2 "Ask yourself, will (the BOK) solely bid up the dollar even more aggressively, while others are talking about China letting the yuan appreciate?" another foreign bank trader said. "You've got to be global."\par \par \uc2 (Seoul-based correspondent Min-Jeong Lee has covered South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2008. She can be reached at 82 2 3700 1908 or min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (In-Soo Nam contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 22, 2010 23:15 ET (03:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100423e64n000hr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
