\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Extends Fall; 31.700-31.770 Band Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2010\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD extends fall to 31.734 vs 31.744 midday, 31.819 yesterday, as foreign investors sell USD to buy local stocks (Taiex ends +1.2%; foreign investors buy net NT$15.23 billion Taiwan shares Thursday), but losses limited on expectations \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will boost pair in late session to slow pair's fall today, says local bank trader. Traders say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected of buying USD yesterday to help exporters. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may tolerate the pair's losses today, as price of U.S. dollar vs Korean won has fallen below key 1,130 level," he says. Adds, pair unlikely to break key 31.700 resistance today on lack of exporters selling as they have sold their USD in late March for settlement needs. Tips pair in 31.700-31.770 band rest of session. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is now within sight of 1.42, and may drop below that level in the short term, though such a move won't induce panic at the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "The SNB has held back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, and is more interested in the pace of CHF appreciation, than any particular level," says analyst Lutz Karpowitz. "The SNB won't panic as consumer prices are rising again and the weak EUR will only have a limited deflationary effect from foreign trade," he adds. EUR/CHF was last at 1.4219. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] Nordea says it sees the best value in the OAT 3.50% April 2020 among the four French government bonds on auction Thursday, as this bond looks attractive on the French curve and also offers pickup versus Finnish and Dutch peers. Senior analyst Jan von Gerich says that French bonds currently trade generally at cheaper levels vs Dutch and Finnish bonds, whereas last year French bonds were notably more expensive. "As the outlook for French public finances is notably darker than that of Finland and of the Netherlands, French bonds may continue to feel pressure," he says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] While difficult to quantify, one of reasons fueling H-shares' relative outperformance vs HSI may be hopes of stronger CNY, which would bode well for wide range of China plays, though exporters, port operators, shippers may take hit. "We see an increasing chance of Renminbi returning to the path of a gradual appreciation against the USD in the next two months," assuming U.S. doesn't provoke Beijing by listing China as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator in bi-annual report due April 15. H-share index +2.0% at 12,647.50 vs HSI +1.1%, rising for 4th session out of five, for cumulative gains of 6.3%. China banks strong with all 7 H-share banks rising more than HSI. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW lower at 1,126.4 late Thursday vs 1,131.3 late yesterday in Seoul as offshore players continue to bet on higher KRW and foreign investors continue to pour funds into local stock market, traders say. But further downside limited for today's spot trade with 2-Seoul based traders suspecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of USD near KRW1,126 in late session, which comes after some traders suspect BOK USD buying near KRW1,128 in early trade. One trader at local bank estimates total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  volume to be around $500 million-$600 million for today. Says, "with so many foreign investors pouring in to the local stock market, (the authorities) won't be able to reverse the pair's downtrend." Another trader tips pair in 1,122-1,132 range for Friday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed on the day, maintaining support after Wednesday's disappointing US ADP jobs data despite data showing business conditions in China and Japan improving. Furthermore, Greece remains a risk in Europe as investors still question the country's ability to finance its debt. German retail sales, released early Thursday, fell 0.4% on the month, versus 0.3% forecast. Still to come, euro zone manufacturing PMIs are due at 0800 GMT while in the US, jobless claims are due at 1230 GMT and manufacturing ISM is due at 1400 GMT. Plus, France sells EUR8.5 billion bonds at auction. The bund yield curve is unchanged on the day, with the 2-year/10-year benchmark spread at +214.3 bps. The June bund futures contract is currently unchanged at on the day at 123.35. The 10-year benchmark bund is unchanged at 101.31, yielding 3.091%. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] Given a series of weaker than expected economic data in March, Citi expects some dovish comments from Poland's Monetary Policy Council members after its Wednesday decision to leave rates on hold at 3.50%. However, these won't fuel any rally in bond yields as the market prices in only one rate hike in late '10, Citi adds. On the contrary, Citi sees risks of an upward move in longer yields. That's because recent structure of government bond holdings shows holdings of foreign investors rose to a record high of PLN92B after Feb, making the domestic debt market more susceptible to global changes in risk aversion, Citi adds. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling amount edges up 2.3% at HK$1.747 billion midday; percentage of market total unchanged vs Wednesday's midday at 4.8%, suggesting bears staying put with HSI rising for 4th session out of five. Only one stock has more than HK$100 million worth of shorts -- A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$103.11 million, followed by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$62.50 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$54.06 million. China Cosco (1919.HK), ICBC (1398.HK) round out top 5, with HK$51.67 million, HK$51.01 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] China's strong March PMI (at 55.1 vs 52.0 in February) suggests the country's industrial production growth in March likely exceeded 20% on-year and real GDP growth likely topped 12.0% in 1Q10, Nomura says. Reiterates forecast of real GDP growth of 10.5% in 2010 and continues to see upside risk to forecast, noting signs manufacturing sector has started an even stronger expansion after the Lunar New Year holidays, as strong orders and sharply rising input prices are encouraging firms to increase their raw materials inventory. "This may deserve close attention from now on as rising input prices and the accumulation of raw materials may reinforce each other, forming a positive feedback cycle over the coming months." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Asian markets higher as data show manufacturing activity in much of region continued to expand in March. Nikkei +1.4%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.7%, Kospi +1.6%, HSI +0.9%, STI 1.2% higher, NZX-50 +0.3%, KLCI +0.8%, Taiex +1.2%, Sensex +0.4%, Shanghai Composite +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD touches 3-month high of 93.65 vs JPY as insurers, institutional investors buy at start of new Japanese fiscal year; USD/JPY last at 93.50 vs 93.46 late Wednesday in New York, EUR/JPY at 126.30 vs 126.28. EUR/USD consolidates recent gains at 1.3511 vs 1.3509. Japanese business sentiment improved; March tankan index recovered to minus 14 vs minus 25 in December. Australia's February trade deficit widened to seasonally adjusted A$1.92 billion vs January's revised A$1.12 billion deficit; Job vacancies +12.1% in seasonally adjusted terms during 3-month period ended Feb. 28, from previous 3-month period; Industry Group-\uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\uc2  Performance of Manufacturing Index for March down 3.6 points on-month at 50.2; TD-Securities-Melbourne Institute Monthly Inflation Gauge +0.5% on-month in March. China's government-backed PMI at 55.1 in March vs 52.0 in February; HSBC China Manufacturing PMI higher at 57.0 in March vs 55.8 previous month. Korea CPI +2.3% on-year, +0.3% on-month, while March exports +35.1% on-year at $37.68 billion, imports +48.4% at $35.49 billion; \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at seasonally adjusted 55.6 last month vs 58.2 in February. Taiwan March PMI at 62.7 vs February's 62.5. Spot gold at $1,112.50/oz, down $1.10 from NY close. Nymex May crude oil futures down 40 cents at $83.36/bbl on Globex. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's March inflation tamer than market forecasts, surprising economists. HI Investment & Securities economist Park Sang-hyun says prices of agricultural products likely didn't rise as much as thought despite cold weather, while price gain in global raw material prices not yet carried over to consumer prices. Economy in better-than-expected shape, but weakness in composite leading index yesterday suggests recovery will be slower in months ahead, says Park; with inflation coming below expectations, no big reason for BOK to raise rates now. Inflation to rise to lower-end of 3% in 3Q partly because government expected to raise utility fare after regional election in June; BOK likely to start tightening in December, adds Park. CPI +2.3% on year, +0.3% on month in March vs +2.6% on-year, +0.6% on-month forecasts; prices +2.7% on year, +0.4% on month in February. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end broadly up, +1.2% at 10-week high of 8013.09, trading volume also at 10-week high of NT$138.42 billion, buoyed by gains in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korean bourses, possibly some government funds \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid Taiwan-\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade pact talks in Taipei, says Hua Nan Securities Investment Management Assistant Vice President Henry Miao. "You can't let (trade pact) talks become a negative cue for the market," he says, tips Taiex support tomorrow at 5-day MA of 7943, cap around 8050. "(The trade pact) will be signed in May or June and stocks likely already reflect expectations for strong first-quarter results," he says; tips Taiex to correct to 7700 through May before trade pact signed. Construction subindex +3.2% after Economic Daily News report that Huaku Development (2548.TW) sold its residential Nangang project in only 10 days, while chip packaging, testing firms boosted by report on price hikes for 2Q: Huaku ends +4.1% to NT$85.80, Goldsun Development (2504.TW) +5.2% to NT$14.10, Advanced Semiconductor (2311.TW) +2.1% to [ 04-01-10 0247ET ]\par \par \uc2 NT$29.60; Hon Hai Precision ends +1.8% to NT$140.0 after buying 90.1% of \uc2 Sony\uc2 's Slovakia LCD TV plant. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com) [ 04-01-10 0247ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100401e6410004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
