\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CHF May Drop Through 1.42; SNB Won't Panic\b0\par\par\uc2 1,899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2010\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is now within sight of 1.42, and may drop below that level in the short term, though such a move won't induce panic at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The SNB has held back on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently, and is more interested in the pace of CHF appreciation, than any particular level," says analyst Lutz Karpowitz. "The SNB won't panic as consumer prices are rising again and the weak EUR will only have a limited deflationary effect from foreign trade," he adds. EUR/CHF was last at 1.4219. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] Nordea says it sees the best value in the OAT 3.50% April 2020 among the four French government bonds on auction Thursday, as this bond looks attractive on the French curve and also offers pickup versus Finnish and Dutch peers. Senior analyst Jan von Gerich says that French bonds currently trade generally at cheaper levels vs Dutch and Finnish bonds, whereas last year French bonds were notably more expensive. "As the outlook for French public finances is notably darker than that of Finland and of the Netherlands, French bonds may continue to feel pressure," he says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] While difficult to quantify, one of reasons fueling H-shares' relative outperformance vs HSI may be hopes of stronger CNY, which would bode well for wide range of China plays, though exporters, port operators, shippers may take hit. "We see an increasing chance of Renminbi returning to the path of a gradual appreciation against the USD in the next two months," assuming U.S. doesn't provoke Beijing by listing China as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator in bi-annual report due April 15. H-share index +2.0% at 12,647.50 vs HSI +1.1%, rising for 4th session out of five, for cumulative gains of 6.3%. China banks strong with all 7 H-share banks rising more than HSI. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW lower at 1,126.4 late Thursday vs 1,131.3 late yesterday in Seoul as offshore players continue to bet on higher KRW and foreign investors continue to pour funds into local stock market, traders say. But further downside limited for today's spot trade with 2-Seoul based traders suspecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of USD near KRW1,126 in late session, which comes after some traders suspect BOK USD buying near KRW1,128 in early trade. One trader at local bank estimates total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  volume to be around $500 million-$600 million for today. Says, "with so many foreign investors pouring in to the local stock market, (the authorities) won't be able to reverse the pair's downtrend." Another trader tips pair in 1,122-1,132 range for Friday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed on the day, maintaining support after Wednesday's disappointing US ADP jobs data despite data showing business conditions in China and Japan improving. Furthermore, Greece remains a risk in Europe as investors still question the country's ability to finance its debt. German retail sales, released early Thursday, fell 0.4% on the month, versus 0.3% forecast. Still to come, euro zone manufacturing PMIs are due at 0800 GMT while in the US, jobless claims are due at 1230 GMT and manufacturing ISM is due at 1400 GMT. Plus, France sells EUR8.5 billion bonds at auction. The bund yield curve is unchanged on the day, with the 2-year/10-year benchmark spread at +214.3 bps. The June bund futures contract is currently unchanged at on the day at 123.35. The 10-year benchmark bund is unchanged at 101.31, yielding 3.091%. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] Given a series of weaker than expected economic data in March, Citi expects some dovish comments from Poland's Monetary Policy Council members after its Wednesday decision to leave rates on hold at 3.50%. However, these won't fuel any rally in bond yields as the market prices in only one rate hike in late '10, Citi adds. On the contrary, Citi sees risks of an upward move in longer yields. That's because recent structure of government bond holdings shows holdings of foreign investors rose to a record high of PLN92B after Feb, making the domestic debt market more susceptible to global changes in risk aversion, Citi adds. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling amount edges up 2.3% at HK$1.747 billion midday; percentage of market total unchanged vs Wednesday's midday at 4.8%, suggesting bears staying put with HSI rising for 4th session out of five. Only one stock has more than HK$100 million worth of shorts -- A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$103.11 million, followed by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$62.50 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$54.06 million. China Cosco (1919.HK), ICBC (1398.HK) round out top 5, with HK$51.67 million, HK$51.01 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] China's strong March PMI (at 55.1 vs 52.0 in February) suggests the country's industrial production growth in March likely exceeded 20% on-year and real GDP growth likely topped 12.0% in 1Q10, Nomura says. Reiterates forecast of real GDP growth of 10.5% in 2010 and continues to see upside risk to forecast, noting signs manufacturing sector has started an even str