\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HK Short Sales +2.3% Midday; Ratio Remains Benign\b0\par\par\uc2 1,841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2010\par 08:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling amount edges up 2.3% at HK$1.747 billion midday; percentage of market total unchanged vs Wednesday's midday at 4.8%, suggesting bears staying put with HSI rising for 4th session out of five. Only one stock has more than HK$100 million worth of shorts -- A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$103.11 million, followed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0005.HK) with HK$62.50 million, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0939.HK) with HK$54.06 million. China Cosco (1919.HK), ICBC (1398.HK) round out top 5, with HK$51.67 million, HK$51.01 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] China's strong March PMI (at 55.1 vs 52.0 in February) suggests the country's industrial production growth in March likely exceeded 20% on-year and real GDP growth likely topped 12.0% in 1Q10, Nomura says. Reiterates forecast of real GDP growth of 10.5% in 2010 and continues to see upside risk to forecast, noting signs manufacturing sector has started an even stronger expansion after the Lunar New Year holidays, as strong orders and sharply rising input prices are encouraging firms to increase their raw materials inventory. "This may deserve close attention from now on as rising input prices and the accumulation of raw materials may reinforce each other, forming a positive feedback cycle over the coming months." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Asian markets higher as data show manufacturing activity in much of region continued to expand in March. Nikkei +1.4%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.7%, Kospi +1.6%, HSI +0.9%, STI 1.2% higher, NZX-50 +0.3%, KLCI +0.8%, Taiex +1.2%, Sensex +0.4%, Shanghai Composite +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD touches 3-month high of 93.65 vs JPY as insurers, institutional investors buy at start of new Japanese fiscal year; USD/JPY last at 93.50 vs 93.46 late Wednesday in New York, EUR/JPY at 126.30 vs 126.28. EUR/USD consolidates recent gains at 1.3511 vs 1.3509. Japanese business sentiment improved; March tankan index recovered to minus 14 vs minus 25 in December. Australia's February trade deficit widened to seasonally adjusted A$1.92 billion vs January's revised A$1.12 billion deficit; Job vacancies +12.1% in seasonally adjusted terms during 3-month period ended Feb. 28, from previous 3-month period; Industry Group-\uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\uc2  Performance of Manufacturing Index for March down 3.6 points on-month at 50.2; TD-Securities-Melbourne Institute Monthly Inflation Gauge +0.5% on-month in March. China's government-backed PMI at 55.1 in March vs 52.0 in February; HSBC China Manufacturing PMI higher at 57.0 in March vs 55.8 previous month. Korea CPI +2.3% on-year, +0.3% on-month, while March exports +35.1% on-year at $37.68 billion, imports +48.4% at $35.49 billion; \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at seasonally adjusted 55.6 last month vs 58.2 in February. Taiwan March PMI at 62.7 vs February's 62.5. Spot gold at $1,112.50/oz, down $1.10 from NY close. Nymex May crude oil futures down 40 cents at $83.36/bbl on Globex. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's March inflation tamer than market forecasts, surprising economists. HI Investment & Securities economist Park Sang-hyun says prices of agricultural products likely didn't rise as much as thought despite cold weather, while price gain in global raw material prices not yet carried over to consumer prices. Economy in better-than-expected shape, but weakness in composite leading index y