\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China March Industrial Output Likely +20% -Nomura\b0\par\par\uc2 1,893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2010\par 08:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] China's strong March PMI (at 55.1 vs 52.0 in February) suggests the country's industrial production growth in March likely exceeded 20% on-year and real GDP growth likely topped 12.0% in 1Q10, Nomura says. Reiterates forecast of real GDP growth of 10.5% in 2010 and continues to see upside risk to forecast, noting signs manufacturing sector has started an even stronger expansion after the Lunar New Year holidays, as strong orders and sharply rising input prices are encouraging firms to increase their raw materials inventory. "This may deserve close attention from now on as rising input prices and the accumulation of raw materials may reinforce each other, forming a positive feedback cycle over the coming months." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Asian markets higher as data show manufacturing activity in much of region continued to expand in March. Nikkei +1.4%, S&P/ASX 200 +0.7%, Kospi +1.6%, HSI +0.9%, STI 1.2% higher, NZX-50 +0.3%, KLCI +0.8%, Taiex +1.2%, Sensex +0.4%, Shanghai Composite +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD touches 3-month high of 93.65 vs JPY as insurers, institutional investors buy at start of new Japanese fiscal year; USD/JPY last at 93.50 vs 93.46 late Wednesday in New York, EUR/JPY at 126.30 vs 126.28. EUR/USD consolidates recent gains at 1.3511 vs 1.3509. Japanese business sentiment improved; March tankan index recovered to minus 14 vs minus 25 in December. Australia's February trade deficit widened to seasonally adjusted A$1.92 billion vs January's revised A$1.12 billion deficit; Job vacancies +12.1% in seasonally adjusted terms during 3-month period ended Feb. 28, from previous 3-month period; Industry Group-\uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\uc2  Performance of Manufacturing Index for March down 3.6 points on-month at 50.2; TD-Securities-Melbourne Institute Monthly Inflation Gauge +0.5% on-month in March. China's government-backed PMI at 55.1 in March vs 52.0 in February; HSBC China Manufacturing PMI higher at 57.0 in March vs 55.8 previous month. Korea CPI +2.3% on-year, +0.3% on-month, while March exports +35.1% on-year at $37.68 billion, imports +48.4% at $35.49 billion; \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at seasonally adjusted 55.6 last month vs 58.2 in February. Taiwan March PMI at 62.7 vs February's 62.5. Spot gold at $1,112.50/oz, down $1.10 from NY close. Nymex May crude oil futures down 40 cents at $83.36/bbl on Globex. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's March inflation tamer than market forecasts, surprising economists. HI Investment & Securities economist Park Sang-hyun says prices of agricultural products likely didn't rise as much as thought despite cold weather, while price gain in global raw material prices not yet carried over to consumer prices. Economy in better-than-expected shape, but weakness in composite leading index yesterday suggests recovery will be slower in months ahead, says Park; with inflation coming below expectations, no big reason for BOK to raise rates now. Inflation to rise to lower-end of 3% in 3Q partly because government expected to raise utility fare after regional election in June; BOK likely to start tightening in December, adds Park. CPI +2.3% on year, +0.3% on month in March vs +2.6% on-year, +0.6% on-month forecasts; prices +2.7% on year, +0.4% on month in February. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end broadly up, +1.2% at 10-week high of 8013.09, trading volume also at 10-week high of NT$138.42 billion, buoyed by gains in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korean bourses, possibly some government funds \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid Taiwan-\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade pact talks in Taipei, says Hua Nan Securities Investment Management Assistant Vice President Henry Miao. "You can't let (trade pact) talks become a negative cue for the market," he says, tips Taiex support tomorrow at 5-day MA of 7943, cap around 8050. "(The trade pact) will be signed in May or June and stocks likely already reflect expectations for strong first-quarter results," he says; tips Taiex to correct to 7700 through May before trade pact signed. Construction subindex +3.2% after Economic Daily News report that Huaku Development (2548.TW) sold its residential Nangang project in only 10 days, while chip packaging, testing firms boosted by report on price hikes for 2Q: Huaku ends +4.1% to NT$85.80, Goldsun Development (2504.TW) +5.2% to NT$14.10, Advanced Semiconductor (2311.TW) +2.1% to NT$29.60; Hon Hai Precision ends +1.8% to NT$140.0 after buying 90.1% of \uc2 Sony\uc2 's Slovakia LCD TV plant. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says CNY revaluation may take place within next 3 months, earlier than previous expectation; also lowers forecast for 3-month USD/CNY NDFs to 6.74 vs 6.83 previously, 6-month NDFs to 6.66 vs 6.83 previously. "Considering the growing risks on inflation and trade protectionism, we believe China will likely benefit from an exchange rate appreciation soon," firm says. Adds, China most likely to widen daily USD/CNY trading band to 1% above or below central parity from current 0.5% band, followed by downward crawl in USD/CNY central parity; also, "a relatively small and symbolic one-off revaluation remains possible, but the likelihood of a more sizeable move remains negligible, in our view." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end mixed in heavy volume, as better-than-expected 30-year reopened bonds auction yesterday, buying by banks offset by gains in Taiwan shares (Taiex ends +1.2%), expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to buoy short-term rates higher, says local bank trader. Notes, Taiwan average interbank overnight rate climbs to multi-month high in late March. Tips bonds in tight ranges in short term on mixed short-term outlooks for bonds; "...this situation is not likely to change until results of negotiable certificates of deposit and certificates of deposit (NCDs/CDs) auction, 5-year reopened government bonds auction come out in following weeks," he says. 10-year yield ends at 1.4390% vs 1.4406%, tipped in 1.4300%-1.4400% band. In when-issued trade, 5-year yield ends at 1.0279% vs 1.0338%, eyed in 1.0100%-1.0350% band. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB futures rising sharply as domestic investors buying cash bonds actively at start of Japan's new fiscal year, says Mizuho Securities market analyst Masashi Shimominami; "As JGB prices had fallen in recent days, many investors appear to be buying JGBs" at beginning of new fiscal year starting Thursday. But adds, further climbs would be limited as players likely to start taking wait-and-see attitude before release of March U.S. jobs data due Friday. Lead JGB futures at 138.64, up 0.42. 10-year yield down 3 bps at 1.360%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia's trade surplus down to $1.70 billion in February vs $2.03 billion month earlier as exports down 3.2% on month to $11.20 billion, with imports up 0.1% to $9.50 billion, the Central Statistics Agency said Thursday; surplus also below $1.85 billion median forecast from Dow Jones poll; still analysts believe lower trade surplus won't derail rupiah as capital inflows likely to continue on Indonesia's relatively sound fundamentals; (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.9% at 802.82 at midday pause in robust trade. Energy big caps dominate activity on higher oil prices while firmer regional bourses bolster mood. Benchmark taking breather after breaking 800-psychological level, hitting almost 22-month-high at 803.29 intra-session, but sentiment still biased upward, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. "The rise is quite sharp, so investors start to hesitate. We may see some profit-taking that will trim gains but eventually, the index should be able to march forward." Resistance put at 810, support at 795. Among top actives, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) +3.5% at THB29.75, PTT (PTT.TH) +3.4% at THB271, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +2.4% at THB152, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +4.3% at THB268. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall despite suspected USD buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around KRW1,128 earlier in day, traders say, as KRW sentiment lifted by strong Kospi (last +1.4% vs +0.8% earlier). They add, there's continued fund-inflow from overseas to local stock market. Pair last down at 1,126.9 vs 1,131.3 late yesterday in Seoul. "When dollar supply exceeds demand like this, the pair can't help it but fall," says local bank trader. Another local bank trader says, "it looks like pair could fall to as low as the 1,125-1,126 levels." But tips high chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by BOK below 1,125. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0533 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's latest hike in national health insurance premium rate to have minimal impact on discretionary spending, though extra labor costs could make SMEs less inclined to pay raises, says ANZ. Premium up at 5.17%, from 4.55% of insured' monthly salaries, starting today, to replenish cash-strapped program with NT$52.2 billion annually. "We think the direct impact of the premium increases on discretionary consumer spending should be minimal, given the small incremental payments, which account for 0.1% of household disposable income," says ANZ. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com) [ 04-01-10 0212ET ]\par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals up across the board as positive sentiment, gains on the SHFE lead to buying ahead of 4-day LME Easter holiday, says trader at European bank in Singapore. LME 3-month nickel reaches highest since May 2008 at $25,130/ton, up $135 with 131 lots done. "Stainless steel production is ramping so the market feels genuinely tight," he says; tips $26,000 minimum target in this rally despite skepticism from some analysts who point to still-high LME inventories, potential increase in nickel supply from Chinese nickel pig-iron. LME 3-month copper up $72.50 at $7,858.50/ton, with 1,224 lots done. SHFE July copper at CNY62,600/ton, up CNY630 helped by positive China PMI data. China's CFLP PMI at 55.1 in March vs February's 52.0, though February result hurt by factory closures during Lunar New Year holiday, poor weather. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 04-01-10 0212ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100401e6410004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
