\par\par \b \uc2 China ex-lawmaker proposes more flexible yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 12:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, March 31 (Reuters) - China should reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate regime to let the yuan move more widely, Cheng Siwei, an influential former lawmaker, said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Cheng suggested that the yuan be allowed to rise or fall by 3 percent and that the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the limits of the band are reached. He did not specify whether the band would be against the dollar or a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under Cheng's proposal, the yuan would be permitted to fluctuate indefinitely, as long as it stays within the prescribed range, instead of being bound by daily limits.\par \par \uc2 Currently, the yuan may move plus or minus 0.5 percent per day against the dollar, and 3.0 percent against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from midpoint rates set each morning by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cheng, whose comments have moved markets in the past, said his suggestion would introduce greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility while maintaining Beijing's control over the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 In July 2005, China scrapped the yuan's dollar peg and adopted a "managed floating exchange rate system with reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 The authorities have not disclosed the composition of the basket or the weight of its constituent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The current practice of 'referring to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' has given a very small role to market signals and the balance of supply and demand," Cheng told a forum in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 He said a rise or fall of less than 3 percent would constitute \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability -- China's mantra even when it permitted the yuan to rise a further 19 percent against the dollar after its initial 2.1 percent revaluation in July 2005.\par \par \uc2 Since July 2008 China has repegged the yuan, also known as the renminbi (RMB), near 6.83 per dollar to help its exporters weather the global downturn.\par \par \uc2 "I read a report saying that a 2 percent appreciation in the yuan might kill China's textile industry. That is certainly an exaggeration," Cheng said.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese-language Caijing magazine on Tuesday quoted unidentified sources as saying Beijing was studying the option of dropping the yuan's peg as early as next month.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  this week joined the ranks of banks expecting China to break the peg before long.\par \par \uc2 "Now that exports have rebounded strongly, we expect the RMB to be allowed to appreciate faster against the dollar in Q2 2010, most likely in the form of a gradual move accompanied by an increase in the daily trading band, and trade at 6.4-6.5 against the dollar by end year," Tao Wang, a \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist in Beijing, said in a report.\par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/YUAN|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|Z|U|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100331e63v0013x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
