\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  To Target Bubbles, Official Says -Nikkei\b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 19:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Nikkei)--\uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  aims to forestall runaway increases in asset prices this year while supporting stable and relatively brisk economic growth, Assistant Governor Guo Qingping told The Nikkei recently in a rare reference to bubbles by a Chinese central banker, the newspaper reported in its Friday morning edition.\par \par \uc2 It suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move faster to correct what has been called excessive monetary easing. Some at the bank appear to share concerns that policies encouraging bank lending have created a severe excess of liquidity that is inflating real estate prices.\par \par \uc2 Many believe that the situation is linked to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable against the dollar. Trends in real estate and consumer prices could affect the timing of the revaluation of the renminbi.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  will continue easing as appropriate in the face of the numerous economic challenges at home and abroad, Guo said in a written statement. But he added that it is necessary to increase the accuracy and flexibility of monetary policy at the same time, suggesting that the bank is ready to make adjustments based on price trends.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 11, 2010 13:33 ET (18:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100311e63b005fo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
