\par\par \uc2 Money Talks\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: China Factor May Prompt Taiwan Curbs On Inflows\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2010\par 09:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Taiwan's authorities are likely to act on recent warnings and impose more measures to curb inflows, in a bid to curtail upward pressure on the Taiwan dollar.\par \par \uc2 China will probably be a factor that forces Taipei's hand, and that will mean in turn the Taiwan dollar continues to underperform its Asian counterparts this year.\par \par \uc2 The upcoming trade pact with Beijing in May, considered a stepping stone towards a comprehensive free trade agreement, is expected to boost investment flows from mainland China. These closer ties, the result of a friendlier political environment after the Nationalist Party won the last presidential election, could potentially lead to asset bubbles if the inflows aren't managed carefully or if they end up being diverted to more speculative parts of the economy.\par \par \uc2 As an open economy, Taiwan is seen by many as a proxy to invest in China's more restricted markets; this is already attracting large inflows, though for now authorities are managing to contain the Taiwan dollar via a combination of administrative measures, warnings to the market and market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But with inflows set to rise even further once the trade pact takes effect, it will add urgency to the measures that have been put in place. Taipei also won't want heavy inflows from China to become a political talking point if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to rise too fast.\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar struggled to match its rivals in the region last year. Then, held back by continued \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it only gained 3% against the U.S. dollar, well below the double digit rallies posted by the Korean won and Indonesian rupiah.\par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone may not suffice to offset inflows from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Taiwan doesn't want to put a halt to inflows, but it will want to manage them carefully, and that may prompt limits on foreign ownership of Taiwan dollar accounts and short-term government bonds, a preferred target for speculators.\par \par \uc2 There is reason for concern. Taiwan has only to look to Hong Kong, where inflows from China have helped drive up property and stock prices, forcing the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject huge amounts of liquidity to keep up with demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to warn against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, and in November imposed a ban on foreign investors from parking funds in time deposits. The bank earlier this month also issued instructions on how long banks should hold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of clients.\par \par \uc2 Over 2009, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose a whopping 20%, and so far this month net portfolio inflows to equities alone are nearing $2 billion, the second largest number in the region.\par \par \uc2 With China clearly in the frame, Taiwan dollar bulls may struggle to rise above policymakers' efforts to fight speculative investments.\par \par \uc2 (David Roman, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (Mark Cranfield contributed to this story)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -0- \par  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 29, 2010 03:31 ET (08:31 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120100129e61t000ze\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
