\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Policy Risks To Trump Econ Risks This Year - Jen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2010\par 02:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] For asset markets, policy risks will become increasingly more important than economic risks in 2010, says BlueGold Capital's Stephen Jen; says in contrast to 2008-09, major economies will likely adopt quite diverse fiscal, monetary policies. U.S. and Europe in particular "will likely have the exact opposite mixes of fiscal and monetary policies, just as China and Japan will have diametrically different policy mixes"; political considerations will likely lead to U.S. maintaining maximum fiscal stimulus in 2010, which in turn could force Fed to be less stimulative, but peripheral EMU nations will be forced by bond markets to retrench fiscally, forcing ECB to maintain monetary stimulus for longer than it otherwise would. "These divergent policy mixes will likely further weigh on the euro/dollar in 2010, exacerbating the effects coming from a prospective growth deficit in Euroland."(RXM)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB higher at 33.03 vs 32.97-32.99 late Friday as risk aversion spikes ahead of expected soft equities markets following Wall Street slide. "The market is still talking about Obama's comments over the banking system and that's hurting equities, so people are seeking a safe haven," says Bangkok-based dealer. Pair tipped in 32.95-33.10 band today.(LHM)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] Though drop in Australian producer prices means little in terms of Wednesday's upcoming CPI report, it makes a big difference for the next two quarters to follow. "Today's data points to further downward pressure on consumer inflation through at least the first half of 2010...providing some hope that core inflation will head down towards the RBA's forecast of 2.25% by end 2010," wrote \uc2 UBS\uc2  in a Monday note. \uc2 UBS\uc2  adds that producer prices often lead consumer prices by roughly two or three quarters, with Monday's report signalling the third decline in four quarters for PPI. (GER)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI set to fall on Wall Street cues, in short term may "overshoot on the downside" to below psychological 20,000, says \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 's Ben Kwong. "Risk aversion is returning given so many uncertainties, both in the U.S. and China"; still, at 20,000, HSI would be trading only tad above 13X this year's consensus earnings, thus "some medium- and long-term investors may be eying to enter at below 20,000." \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) likely to lead falls, tracking weakness in U.S. peers, while Bank of China's (3988.HK) big fund-raising plan set to weigh on stock. HSI ends down 0.7% at 20,726.18 Friday, falling for 8th session out of past 9, for cumulative loss of 7.5%.(RLI)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian PPI will likely take some pressure off Wednesday's all important 4Q inflation data, but correlation between the two not that strong, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Economist Annette Beacher; "it is problematic and not especially helpful to translate this PPI report for Wednesday's CPI report, as the difference between the two being margins decided by individual businesses." Still, says strong AUD impact on PPI will be seen in headline CPI; "the mix of PPI inflation is likely to be reflected in the headline CPI, with imported inflation providing an offset to sticky inflation elsewhere, and we are comfortable forecasting CPI below consensus."(EGC)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may fall, taking cues from weak U.S. stocks, with Shanghai Composite Index likely to test technical support of 3100 after ending down 1.0% at 3128.59 Friday, says Jacky Zhang at Capital Securities. Also, "concerns over a supply glut of new shares will continue to discourage investors," he says; notes 7 companies will start to build IPO books this week, including China First Heavy Industries' more than CNY8 billion share offering. Still, Bank of China (601988.SH) may not fall sharply though over weekend it announced CNY40 billion of convertible bond sale; bank also seeks board approval for mandate to sell up to 20% new shares (both H-, A-shares), potentially raising more than CNY200 billion. Analysts say Bank of China's fundraising plan has been priced in, share offering less likely to take place before bond sale, tip support at CNY4.00, stock ends up 0.7% at CNY4.16 Friday. Shenzhen Index off 2.7% at 1161.89 Friday. (RWY)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] Lower-than-expected Australian PPI figure for 4Q pleasing for market from inflationary standpoint, but not enough to change expectations going into Wednesday's heavily watched CPI report. "I'm surprised it was down, likely dominated by the impact of a high Aussie dollar," said David De Garis, senior economist for \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 . "It's helpful to quell some inflationary pressures but I'd caution against drawing too many conclusions against CPI." Notably, De Garis says he won't be revising his CPI forecast for Wednesday, when he expects the ABS to report a 0.5% increase. (GER)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP expected to gain further ground after settling at 46.19 late Friday, buoyed by risk aversion sparked by U.S. President Obama's proposed bank restrictions; pair may rise to initial resistance at 46.30, with next stop at 46.50, support at 46.00; "We'll likely see a gap up (for the dollar) because of the Obama (proposal) concern, and the weaker equities," says local bank trader. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to rise in 31.965-32.050 band on foreign fund outflows amid expected losses in local shares, but gains likely limited on exporter selling, says local bank trader. Notes central's bank anticipated meeting with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders today to discuss TWD forwards likely to weigh on TWD; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to make sure that exporters' \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards demand is not based on speculation." But notes impact likely limited as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to reiterate existing rules. Pair at 31.980 late Friday.(WUM)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY likely to continue benefiting from uncertainty over any further monetary policy tightening in China, though any boosts to Japanese unit from CNY rises to remain fleeting as driven by unfounded speculation of link that doesn't exist, says Masafumi Yamamoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Following string of tightening measures in China since start of month, says investors to continue turning to safe-haven JPY since "it makes sense for (them) to reduce risk positions, considering the growing uncertainty - the timing, pace and method of tightening, and its effect on asset markets and economies." Adds, JPY stands to gain most vs risk-sensitive counterparts like AUD, EUR. But says despite talk about CNY appreciation lifting JPY, no clear link exists beyond short-term, speculatively driven one; so says "we see it as a good opportunity to buy the USD/JPY on dips if the JPY happens to appreciate following the rising expectation of CNY appreciation." (ATM)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT \u166\'00\'A6Dow Jones\u168\'00\'A8 LME 3-month copper settled almost 1.6% higher Friday at US$7,390/metric ton, after finding support from fund buying. According to \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  technical analysis, copper now hovering on confluence of support around gap in range $7,040/$7,170, which ideally should hold firm early this week; but if this gives way would extend bearish bias, imply return to near $6,500, possibly lower; says rally to above $7,590 needed to return focus to topside, though this an unlikely scenario this week. Medium term, consolidation above $7,170 keeps copper on course to test $8,000. (RCB)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura says possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , interest rate increases, potential fund outflows, impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening should "only trigger a temporary pause" in HK residential property market, but not cause home prices to fall. Instead, driven by good affordability, tight vacancies, rising rents, expects home prices to rise 20% over next 2 years; notes whether this 20% gets front- or back-loaded between 2010, 2011 uncertain, but with developers' P/B at 1.25X, right in-line with historical average of 1.22X, "there are no expectations baked into share prices." Tips developers' NAVs to grow by 15% to end-FY11, coupled with past three months' 6% correction, potential upside to price targets reopened to 23% on average. Top sector picks \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), Cheung Kong (0001.HK), \uc2 Kerry Properties\uc2  (0683.HK), \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK).(RLI) [ 01-24-10 2026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100125e61p00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
