\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD Mixed As Rate Fever Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 1,316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2010\par 09:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] Europe opens to a mixed USD with interest rate concerns seemingly taking over from risk worries. China started the ball rolling Tuesday by ratcheting up its RRR rate by 50bp to 16%. Then Philly Fed president Plosser said rates may have to rise before unemployment reaches an acceptable level and Dallas Fed's Fisher warned of the dangers of leaving rates too low for too long. BOE MPC member Sentance said rates may have to rise should inflation surface. Net result, equities slumped with the DJIA shedding 0.3%, the Nikkei 1.3% and the Shanghai Composite a lumpy 3.1%. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets reaction was less aggressive, EUR/USD barely budged, USD/JPY recovered and GBP/USD went bid. For Wednesday the main European bourses are giving no clues, essentially trading flat so it may be a long drawn out session waiting for the U.S. to install some excitement. (GST)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0834 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says "we fear" weak demand for Germany's EUR6 billion of 2.50% February 2015 federal note, or bobl, at Wednesday's auction, saying that the new paper is "dear" relative to its curve. Adds that the bobl is also rich in cross market spreads. "Investors might instead prefer bonds with attractive pick-up, like the new 5-year [Italian] BTP issued at the same time," SocGen says. German bobl auction result is due at 1000 GMT. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is roughly stable Wednesday morning and its direction will depend on the EUR/USD pair, a trader says. Adds the zloty is seen in the EUR/PLN 4.05-4.10 range. EUR/PLN now 4.0741 from 4.0776 late Tuesday, USD/PLN 2.8142 from 2.8111. Average yield of 2-year treasury bonds flat at 5.07%, 5-year flat at 5.70%, 10-year flat at 6.08%. (MMS)\par \par \uc2 0821 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.3500 vs yesterday's 3.3400; dealer says ringgit lower on concerns over global economic recovery after China raises bank reserve requirements. "The immediate reaction was negative on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but seems to have been overdone...we are now seeing the ringgit stabilize and don't expect it to fall very much further in the short-term," says dealer. Tips 3.3350-3.3600 range Thursday. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] China's unexpectedly swift monetary tightening may tip the scales in favor of early rate hikes in other Asian countries, boosting such \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as INR, KRW. PBOC said Tuesday banks must set aside more cash as reserves, a bid to tighten credit and a likely prelude to rate hikes; may be a key consideration for other central banks gauging when to tighten policy as the global economy rebounds. Asian central banks are widely expected to raise rates over the next three quarters to preempt mounting inflationary pressures due to higher commodity prices. India and South Korea have widely been expected to raise rates this quarter or next at the latest. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  next meets Jan. 29 and the Bank of Korea Feb. 11. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: China Tightening May Hasten Asia Rate Hikes (DRB)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may place further restrictions on foreign funds inflows, intended to slow down pace of TWD's rise, after Tuesday sending another "strong signal" to speculators, says Standard Chartered. Notes, late 2009 Taiwan banned foreign funds from parking cash in TWD-time deposits, next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ban foreign buying of government bonds, where lots of foreign money suspected of being parked. No update data for foreign holding of government paper, but "we believe the potential impact of any measures to limit these holdings could be quite substantial," based on 2008 data of NT$180 billion, house says. Adds, any imposition of capital control-type measures may also reverse recent gains in TWD vs USD, also lead to flatter yield curve as foreign money believed parked in short-end bonds. (ABP)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are lower Wednesday and despite Tuesday's strong gains, "It would take a close above the 122.71/85 gap to turn the outlook bullish again and consider a rise at least to the November 2009 high of 124.06," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  technical analysts. Resistance stands at 122.23, 122.45 and 122.71/122.85 while support comes at 121.73/121.74, 121.60 and 121.31. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0815 GMT [Dow Jones] In the fight for supremacy between rates and risk, neither appears to be winning, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. Over the last few weeks, interest rate differentials, the traditional driver of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs, were firmly back in the driving seat. The recent financial crisis appeared to be over and investors were once again trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they had done before. However, last Friday's disappointing U.S. non-farm payrolls has brought that all to a shuddering halt. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD up at 31.809 late vs 31.790 yesterday, tracking gains in USD vs Asians, losses in local shares (Taiex down 1.4%), narrowing offshore NDF discounts, says local bank trader. "Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's warning against hot money, I don't see a lot of foreign funds exiting the market," on expectations China's QDII fund inflows, after cross-straits Financial MOU takes effect Jan 16, will boost TWD further. But notes foreign investors may be more cautious about buying more TWD after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned yesterday it's watching hot money, possible asset price bubbles. Tips pair in 31.750-31.900 band tomorrow. (CMK)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian annual CPI is forecast, in a poll by Dow Jones Newswires of 14 private bank economists, to have accelerated in December to 5.6%, from 5.2% in November, due mostly to sharply higher gas prices versus a year earlier. December expectations are down from a midyear forecast of nearly 8% for end-'09 CPI. Analysts will watch heating and unprocessed food price developments. Clothing prices could stage a surprise as sales started pre-Christmas this year, unlike last year. A lower-than-expected CPI reading would firm rate cut expectations. Data due at 0800 GMT Thursday. (MAF)\par \par \uc2 0804 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 12 rises to 122 bps from Tuesday's 118, 117 bps as concerns grow that China's monetary tightening could gradually make nation's economic activity sluggish, in turn choking off recovery in Japan's export-driven economy, says Akira Nomura, credit analyst at Mizuho Securities. "If demand for Japanese goods recedes in China, that will likely raise fears about the outlook for Japan's exports and the broad economy. Under such circumstances, investors may keep buying CDS and the iTraxx could extend climbs for the time being." Among single-name CDS deals on Wednesday, Promise Co. (8574.TO) at 600 bps vs 1000 bps on Nov. 20; Japan's sovereign CDS USD-denominated at 74.5 bps vs 70, 72 bps on Dec. 22. (TTA)\par \par \uc2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/Asian could rebound in 1H due to market repositioning, but likely to weaken in long term, says Standard Chartered. "Given the capital inflows and current account surpluses, most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to strengthen. However, the pace of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation could be slowed by official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Global Head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research Callum Henderson. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to make a move around mid-2010 to allow greater yuan flexibility, a factor that could support regional units in 2H. "In particular, many countries will look to the Chinese yuan in deciding their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy." (PPP) [ 01-13-10 0335ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100113e61d00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
