\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: China: To Keep Way Of Managing  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2009\par 11:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve management strategy.)\par \par \uc2 By Terence Poon and Li Liu\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China on Friday sought to reassure the outside world it is taking a conservative approach to managing its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, with the government saying it hasn't made big changes to its strategy and the U.S. dollar remains a central asset.\par \par \uc2 China holds the world's biggest reserves of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --US$2.27 trillion at the end of September--and so is under intense scrutiny from both global investors concerned about its moves in financial markets and Chinese citizens worried about their nation's wealth.\par \par \uc2 The statements Friday by a senior regulator, later supplemented by a book published by the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seem intended to reassure investors that Beijing isn't selling its vast holdings of U.S. Treasurys, despite worries the dollar is likely to depreciate in the future.\par \par \uc2 "The composition remains as it was before. There is no major change" in the reserves, said Wang Xiaoyi, vice director of SAFE, which oversees the reserves.\par \par \uc2 "We are not making any big adjustments in direction. Our \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still as usual, meaning they follow the existing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves management goal," he said Friday on the sidelines of an economic forum in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 China has repeatedly said it follows the principles of keeping the reserves safe and liquid, as well as increasing the value of the reserves.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, SAFE posted a Chinese-language book about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues on its Web site. The normally publicity-shy organization said the unusual move was an attempt to increase transparency on how it manages its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings.\par \par \uc2 "A stable, diversified structure of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the U.S. dollar as the main component but with some appropriate diversification, is able to meet our needs of external payments and asset allocation," SAFE said in a chapter of the book on reserve management. It didn't give any precise details on how China's reserves are invested.\par \par \uc2 With the dollar's sharp slide in recent months, academics and officials outside SAFE have stepped up calls for more diversification of the reserves into other investments such as gold and commodities. But SAFE stressed in the book it is inappropriate to invest a large portion of the reserves in volatile and illiquid assets.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves must maintain enough liquidity," it said in the book. "Not only must they satisfy the usual demands of external payments, such as importing necessary materials and technology and encouraging companies to expand abroad, they must also play the role of ensuring the nation's financial security when there may be rapid capital outflows and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under heavy pressure."\par \par \uc2 Senior Chinese officials, including Premier Wen Jiabao, have repeatedly expressed concern about the dollar this year. China, which holds most of its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in dollar assets, worries massive U.S. government borrowing could eventually result in inflation, hurting the value of China's holdings.\par \par \uc2 Wang acknowledged recent declines in the dollar, saying the "depreciation of the U.S. dollar is a long-term trend." In the near term, however, the dollar's key role as the global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't change, SAFE said in the book, which repeated views senior officials have expressed in the past.\par \par \uc2 The size of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves continues to build, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold down the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Wang told Dow Jones Newswires that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves expanded monthly in November and October from the prior month. Official reserve data are only available until end-September.\par \par \uc2 -By Terence Poon and Li Liu, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 8400 7713; li.liu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Victoria Ruan and Andrew Batson contributed to this story.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 04, 2009 05:34 ET (10:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091204e5c4001tm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
