\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Let's Play The  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Speculation Game\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Casey   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2009\par 15:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The late Wim Duisenberg, Jean-Claude Trichet's predecessor as European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, never mastered how to discuss the hypothetical possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without hinting at real action.\par \par \uc2 When reporters pressed him, he'd say things like, "The only time I'll ever mention \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is after we've \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Aha, would come the "gotcha" response, "you just mentioned it," to which Duisenberg would say something about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  being one "tool in the ECB's toolbox."\par \par \uc2 On Oct. 22, 2000, the ECB did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And so did the U.S. and other Group of Seven central banks, each selling dollars in favor of a then-struggling euro.\par \par \uc2 Over the following month, as Duisenberg flip-flopped between ruling out more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and leaving the door open to it, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost four more cents. But right then it established its historical floor and began a nine-year, 78% rally against the dollar. Either Duisenberg got lucky, or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worked.\par \par \uc2 Now, more than ever since then, policymakers' views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are fueling speculation again. This time, however, it's because the euro - and the Canadian dollar, and the Brazilian real and just about every other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - is rallying against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 48 hours, senior officials from Canada, South Africa, Norway, South Korea and New Zealand have warned of the contractionary effect of their strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Canadian officials have gone so far as to mention the I-word. This is not something central bankers do lightly--look at the stress it caused the chain-smoking Duisenberg.\par \par \uc2 So, is coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  planned? Might speculation over this explain the dollar's otherwise inexplicable rebound this week?\par \par \uc2 Any meaningful move to strengthen the dollar must involve the ECB and the Federal Reserve, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair accounts for more than a quarter of the $3.2 trillion-a-day \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. And for now, U.S. and euro-zone officials are offering no Duisenberg-like hints.\par \par \uc2 But then the U.S. Treasury, which decides on such things, never mentions "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " anyway. And in this case, unlike in 2000, the move wouldn't contradict the standard "strong dollar" line.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, although Europeans have been relatively subdued in their comments about the weaker dollar, they have complained about another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : the yuan. Trichet himself said China, which pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, is passing too much appreciation pressure from a weaker greenback onto the euro.\par \par \uc2 Conventional wisdom holds that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works only when exchange rates depart from fundamentals. And there are plenty of fundamental reasons for a weak dollar--not least the gap between the Fed's near-zero interest rates and those of other central banks, including Norway's, which raised rates Tuesday. But there are few fundamental reasons China, with its booming economy and giant trade surplus, should have a weak yuan.\par \par \uc2 So any dollar-bolstering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  really would be targeted at the yuan. If \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't or won't revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , European countries that are still battling deflationary headwinds may have no choice but to act anyway.\par \par \uc2 In fact, China might want them to. It would maintain its hot money-blocking capital controls but also would get a de facto appreciation against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help it wean off export dependence.\par \par \uc2 In this new Group-of-20 world, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to be consulted over any multilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But there's a quasi-precedent for that: Last October, when G-7 central banks announced coordinated rate cuts, the Bank of China simultaneously announced the same.\par \par \uc2 One thing's certain: If the dollar returns to its downward path, both the speculation and the official rhetoric over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will grow--just as it did with Duisenberg in 2000.\par \par \uc2 (Michael Casey, a special writer with Dow Jones Newswires, writes a regular column about the fixed income market. Previously he was Newswires' Buenos Aires bureau chief and before that, assistant managing editor for the U.S. economy, Treasurys and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  group in New York. He can be reached at +1 212 416 2209 or via email at michael.j.casey@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 29-10-09 1415GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091029e5at001td\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
