\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Too Early To Remove Economic Stimulus, China Official Says\b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2009\par 02:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 24 Sep 2009 20:32 EDT *DJ  World Economy Not Completely Out Of Woods -\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Official\par \par \uc2 24 Sep 2009 20:37 EDT *DJ  Govt \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Shouldn't Stay Forever, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Official Says\par \par \uc2 24 Sep 2009 20:37 EDT *DJ  Time To Mull Exit Strategy Proposals, Timing -China Official\par \par \uc2 24 Sep 2009 20:41 EDT *DJ  China: Must Still Support Major Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Stability\par \par \uc2 24 Sep 2009 21:31 EDT DJ  Too Early To Remove Economic Stimulus, China Official Says\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Min Zeng \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PITTSBURGH (Dow Jones)--It is still too early to remove economic stimulus as the world economy is showing only "primitive" signs of recovery, a senior Chinese official said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The world economy is not completely out of the woods," Ma Xin of the National Development and Reform Commission, which steers Chinese economic policy, said at a news briefing Thursday evening on the sidelines of the Group of 20 nations' summit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, he said that government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to revitalize the economy should not stay forever, adding that policy makers should study proposals and timing to remove the stimulus, the exit strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Xie Duo, a senior official from the People's Bank of China, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , also said that given the financial crisis and economic downturn, there is still a need to support the stability of major reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar's role as the world's primary reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been questioned recently and many Chinese officials have expressed concern about the weakness of the dollar, which could hurt their massive dollar-denominated assets that include more than $800 billion in Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan said earlier this year that he hoped there could be reform of the international monetary system, pushing for replacement of the dollar as a leading reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the special drawing rights, a quasi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  used as an accounting unit for the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Min Zeng, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2229; min.zeng@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 24, 2009 21:31 ET (01:31 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090925e59p00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
